import"./NZTpNUN0.js";import"./BkvxQF8z.js";import{t as i,P as n,q as r}from"./D3cAVrQp.js";var a=i("<h1>Adaptive Universal Basic Income (AUBI) Framework v2.6</h1> <hr> <p><strong>Tier:</strong> 1 — Foundational Social Framework<br> <strong>Status:</strong> Representation-authority and person-continuity conformant, observability-hardened, Indigenous-guided, Treaty-integrated synthesis<br> <strong>Revision:</strong> 2.6 (2026-08-21)<br> <strong>Responsible:</strong> Björn Kenneth Holmström, with Indigenous-guided and AI-assisted development<br> <strong>Primary role:</strong> The social compact for unconditional material security, payout architecture, contribution recognition, and the epistemic rights governing how any of it is measured</p> <h3>Related frameworks</h3> <ul><li><strong>Financial Systems Framework</strong> — Hearts, Leaves, Treasury, reserves, redemption, ICTL, NARP, and financial observability</li> <li><strong>Nested Economies Framework</strong> — economic subsidiarity, cross-scale interfaces, compacts, and protected non-convertibility</li> <li><strong>Work in Liberation Framework</strong> — labour governance, Community Providers, and voluntary contribution pathways</li> <li><strong>Adaptive Tax Framework</strong> — revenue architecture and fiscal instruments</li> <li><strong>Meaningful Existence as a Social Right</strong> — purpose-seeking, cultural participation, and freedom beyond employment</li> <li><strong>Community Support Systems</strong> — care, disability, public-service, and mutual-aid interfaces</li> <li><strong>Kintsugi Protocol</strong> — community care, survivor sovereignty, and the care side of release and institutional transitions</li> <li><strong>Value Creation Framework</strong> — recognition of contribution outside wage employment</li> <li><strong>Indigenous & Traditional Knowledge Governance Framework</strong> — cultural sovereignty, FPIC, and protected unobservability</li> <li><strong>Integrated Meta-Governance Framework v1.6.1</strong> — cross-domain coordination, council formation, representation/composition status, and institutional-selection boundaries</li> <li><strong>Treaty for Our Only Home</strong> — legal, fiscal, justice, and emergency authority</li> <li><strong>Constitutional Interface Specification v0.2.6</strong> — purpose-limited person recognition, cross-domain continuity, correction/collision, and external-status interfaces</li> <li><strong>Aurora Accord v1.3.2</strong> — identity-function separation, anti-linkage, minimum disclosure, civil-identity boundary, and data-governance constraints</li></ul> <h3>Version history</h3> <ul><li><strong>2.6 (2026-08-21):</strong> Withdrawal-asymmetry revision. Adds §5.1.3 governing reduction, interruption, and exit of the Layer 1 baseline. Names an asymmetry previously left implicit: adding a baseline and removing one are not mirror operations, because a household that has arranged housing, care, work, and dependants around a baseline is exposed by its removal in a way a household that never received it is not. Policy-level reductions now require a State Estimate Record, published reason and reversal condition, a notice period, phasing above a pre-published threshold, non-retroactivity, and a floor on overpayment recovery. Individual interruption is restricted to grounds published in advance, with an explicit bar on suspension for non-response, dormancy, attestation lapse, open anomaly investigation, non-participation in any process, criminal record or pending proceeding, or refusal of assessment. Custody and institutional residence permit adjustment where a competent authority meets the same needs, with reinstatement effective on the day of release rather than on application and non-reinstatement treated as an unresolved AUBI duty. Pilot termination is classified as a reduction, requiring termination planning in the pilot’s constituting decision. Adds a Layer 1 disbursement-cadence specification, one §5.1 safeguard reference, and one §7.5 transition-shock row. AUBI acquires no custodial, supervisory, or compliance relationship, holds no condition of release, and creates no new observation channel or record type.</li> <li><strong>2.5.3 (2026-08-10):</strong> Capitalization-observation and deferred-mechanism correction patch following the financial-function authority audit of the economic cluster. Adds a housing-cost capitalization row to the §7.5 transition-shock table, pointing to the Housing capitalization observation (Housing §10.1), and adds the same observation to the §5.1 Layer 1 safeguards where Layer 1 is deployed at scale in a housing market. Names a feedback path previously left implicit: the Layer 1 regional calculation weights housing at 40% of its price index, so where a transfer capitalizes into rent, index recalculation partly chases an increase the transfer contributed to; a rising housing component in a market receiving Layer 1 at scale should therefore be accompanied by capitalization observation rather than treated as ordinary price movement. AUBI observes nothing new itself and acquires no housing, land, rent-setting, or credit instrument; remedy selection remains with Bioregional Polis, Adaptive Tax, competent rent and land-use authorities, or external law. Corrects three references treating sovereign-debt conversion as an available mechanism: the §7.5 fiscal-stress mitigation is restated as time-limited debt-service pauses, liquidity facilities, or restructuring under Treaty Article 19.4 and valid financial protocols; the §7.4 Phase 2 stage note and the §1 Ecosystem Integration Note no longer cite sovereign-debt transformation as a distributed architectural defence. Financial Systems §15.8 withdrew the debt-for-care/ecology mechanism and Treaty Article 19.3 defers it pending a dedicated protocol, and no AUBI transition buffer may rest on it. This revision is additive and corrective and alters no payout, layer, LMCI, observability, epistemic-rights, person-continuity, representation, or governance semantics. No housing, land, credit, or debt-conversion authority is created.</li> <li><strong>2.5.2 (2026-08-08):</strong> Representation-authority conformance patch to Integrated Meta-Governance v1.6.1 §3.7. Reclassifies the Social Resilience Council’s inherited 40/30/20/10 composition, BAZ nomination routes, Earth Council temporary appointment, and Indigenous-seat supermajority as non-self-executing design assumptions rather than constitutional rules. Replaces them with representation requirements that the SRC’s own valid constituting charter must translate into lawful composition and selection. Indigenous participation is routed through affected peoples’ own authority rather than a universal GGF quota or proxy-ratification body. Selection does not create jurisdiction; representation is not FPIC or consent. Numeric composition, term, turnover, vacancy, and heightened voting rules may be adopted only by the competent constituting authority. No electoral-administration machinery or new council authority is created.</li> <li><strong>2.5.1 (2026-08-08):</strong> Person-continuity and purpose-limited-attestation conformance patch to CIS v0.2.4 and Aurora v1.3.2. Retires the former <strong>Love Ledger Native ID</strong> concept in favor of an <strong>AUBI Access Attestation</strong> valid only for AUBI enrollment, continuity, and payout access. Clarifies that Community Attestation supplies bounded evidence rather than general legal identity; removes generic portable-identity claims; makes cross-domain same-person recognition separately authorized and non-transitive; adds mistaken-merge, mistaken-split, duplicate, collision, correction, and unlinking requirements; and permits AUBI to consume externally established death or other status changes only for AUBI consequences. Death or inactivity does not erase accrued claims, appeals, recovery, unresolved payment duties, or competent succession questions. Love Ledger participation, recognition, contribution history, biometrics, or AUBI enrollment do not create general civil identity, citizenship, migration status, voting status, justice standing, employment identity, healthcare identity, or a universal correlation key. No civil-registration or universal-identity authority is created.</li> <li><strong>Editorial edition (2026-08-02).</strong> No substantive change. Repaired stale cross-references to frameworks that renumbered after this revision was drafted: the NARP objection pathway now cites Financial Systems §12 (was §14.3), Heartstarter cites Financial Systems §5 (was §4.3), and Phase 2 infrastructure cites Financial Systems §4.3 (was §4.2). Repointed Genesis Protocol references from a numbered-Principle and Roman-numeral scheme that Genesis v0.9 no longer uses: Accountable Coordination and its Audit now cite Integrated Meta-Governance §2.1 and Appendix I, where they are actually defined; the Conscience & Sovereignty Clause cites Genesis §1.5; the Failure Library cites Genesis §4.6; the Legitimacy Drift Detector row is restated as the Founding Intent Record and mandate review at Genesis §16.4–16.5. Two mechanisms cited here no longer exist upstream — the Permeation Mandate and the Deliberative Narrative Protocol — and carry visible editorial notes pending a decision rather than an invented mapping. Removed version pins from the related-frameworks list and body prose; editions consulted are recorded in this version history instead. Replaced opaque repository identifiers (STR-201, TAK-103, STR-205, STR-206, STR-230) with the framework names they stand for, and removed the entry that pointed at a framework already listed by name. Normalised headings to plain text without bold or trailing periods, matching the other economic frameworks, and converted front-matter metadata from heading syntax to plain bold lines. Editions consulted: Financial Systems v3.4; Nested Economies v3.2; Work in Liberation v2.0; Genesis Protocol v0.9; Integrated Meta-Governance v1.5; Treaty for Our Only Home v1.3.</li> <li><strong>2.5 (2026-08-01):</strong> Observability and epistemic-governance revision. Preserved the LMCI-M/LMCI-A firewall while separating both from operational telemetry and the Love Ledger Narrative Feed. Added the Decision Observability Protocol (§4.5), Observation Contracts, State Estimate Records, individual epistemic rights, sensor-change governance, and intervention-sequencing rules. Rebuilt Layer 3 as distinct needs, equity, mobility, reparations, and negative-adjustment pathways. Replaced the direct low-velocity inference with a structured diagnostic (§7.4), added multi-timescale LMCI architecture (§8), and created Appendix G templates and sentinels. Changes are grounded in the AUBI Observability Audit v0.1 and the Low Hearts Velocity falsification harness v0.1–v0.3.</li> <li><strong>2.4 (2026-04-16):</strong> Post-critique resilience enhancements. Added “If You Only Implement 30%” minimum viable guidance (§7.6). Strengthened cross-references to Office of the Adversary, Deliberative Narrative Protocol, and Planetary Immune System. Added explicit acknowledgment of irreducible tensions: cultural renewal, adversarial adaptation, and optimization vs. sacredness (§12.1). No new architectural components; emphasis and accessibility improvements only.</li> <li><strong>2.3 (2026-04-15):</strong> Strengthened cross-references to other frameworks.</li> <li><strong>2.2 (2026-03-10):</strong> Added a dedicated subsection specifying: selection mechanism (BAZ-proportional election + Indigenous Wisdom Council reserved seats), term limits, conflict-of-interest rules, and a constitutional dissolution/reformation trigger (e.g., if a supermajority of BAZs request review). Fixed circular LMCI dependency. Add Hearts bootstrapping protocol</li> <li><strong>2.1 (2025-12-21):</strong> Added “Preface: Terminology & Intent” to bridge radical and professional language registers while maintaining analytical clarity.</li> <li><strong>2.0 (2025-12-09):</strong> Security Patch</li> <li><strong>1.9 (2025-11-05):</strong> Aligned with Maria Karlova’s analysis of the parasistic control system of civilization.</li> <li><strong>1.7 (2025-09-03):</strong> Aligned with “The Currency of Care” white paper: Introduced dual system of monetized Hearts economy and non-monetized Love Ledger; restructured payout architecture to separate Love Ledger from Hearts verification; added Hearts Treasury subsection; integrated Grassroots Bootstrapping Pathway; enhanced Community Weavers details.</li> <li><strong>1.6 (2025-07-06):</strong> Synthesized v4.1 with v2.11 and v3.0. Retained v4.1’s structure and Indigenous-guided philosophy. Integrated detailed appendices from v2.11 (Failure Recovery, Political Economy, Technical Architecture, Case Studies) and v3.0’s implementation sequencing, pilot site selection matrix, and stress testing protocols. Injected youth internship details and LMCI validation specifics. Relocated Pandemic Resilience, Space Economy Bridge, and Interfaith Governance to respective frameworks.</li> <li><strong>1.5 (2025-07-06):</strong> Integrated Community Weaver role in Section 7.3, replacing Flourishing Guides, with compensation via Layer 2 Hearts bonuses. Linked Green Job Score to Leaves rewards in Section 5.2, as determined by Fractal Labor Parliament (FLP). Clarified Social Resilience Council’s mandate in Section 4.1 as fiscal oversight and equitable distribution, distinct from FLP’s role in setting valuation standards. Maintained Indigenous-guided principles and Treaty integration.</li> <li><strong>1.4 (2025-07-04):</strong> Deepened integration with Indigenous & Traditional Knowledge Governance Framework, reframed AUBI as an expression of Treaty’s social contract, redefined Layer 0 as philosophical foundation, introduced Hearts/Leaves as lifeblood with dual-stream baseline, positioned Social Resilience Council within Meta-Governance, and incorporated Right Relationship in LMCI.</li> <li><strong>1.3 (2025-06-16):</strong> Claude revision: Added implementation sequencing details, economic stress testing protocols, governance operationalization, cultural integration deepening, technical architecture enhancement, Hearts currency evolution, LMCI measurement refinement, Layer 0 operationalization, pilot site selection criteria, and failure cascade prevention mechanisms.</li> <li><strong>1.2 (2025-06-16):</strong> Added Minimum Viable System summary concept, separated core mechanisms from exploratory R&D, centralized governance model with diagram, added business case for Hearts currency, reorganized appendices thematically, enhanced integration with Love Ledger and Hearts currency.</li> <li><strong>1.1 (2025-06-16):</strong> Added LMCI + AUBI Dashboard UX outline, Layer 2 dynamic cap adjustment, gamified feedback interface for contribution tracking, optional Layer 0 concept, and shortform public summary flyer concept for future drafting.</li> <li><strong>1.0 (2025-06-16):</strong> Added LMCI cultural veto, Hearts currency stabilization, contribution decay rule, corporate LMCI incentives, AUBI Mythology toolkit, clarified secular mindfulness as spiritual care, specified AI productivity thresholds for automation tax.</li> <li><strong>0.9 (2025-06-16):</strong> Enhanced economic modeling, detailed technical implementation, added political economy strategies, refined LMCI measurement, specified Flourishing Guide training, strengthened failure recovery protocols.</li> <li><strong>0.8 (2025-06-16):</strong> Integrated Love, Meaning, and Connection Index (LMCI), reframed contribution categories, detailed negative adjustment process, expanded Support Worker role as Flourishing Guides, added AUBI-LMCI flowchart.</li> <li><strong>0.7 (2025-06-16):</strong> Added interactive visuals, Decolonial Oversight Council, youth civic internships, Hearts currency dominance scenario, Universal Earth Service vision, AI feedback dashboard, “AUBI for Humanity” narrative.</li> <li><strong>0.6 (2025-06-16):</strong> Added failure recovery protocols, LMCI validation criteria, enhanced capital flight mitigation, Support Worker burnout prevention, cultural red teaming, AUBI Patriotism framing.</li> <li><strong>0.5 (2025-06-16):</strong> Added Meaningful Existence Index, deepened economic analysis, detailed implementation timeline, enhanced cultural adaptation protocols, strengthened safeguards, communication strategy.</li> <li><strong>0.4 (2025-06-16):</strong> Added negative adjustment mechanism, clarified Support Worker role, elaborated Hearts currency benefits, financing flowchart, version history.</li> <li><strong>0.3 (2025-06-16):</strong> Integrated Treaty mechanisms, diverse case studies, Cultural Adaptation Module.</li> <li><strong>0.2 (2025-06-16):</strong> Added Treaty dependency matrix, spiritual care contributions, macroeconomic modeling.</li> <li><strong>0.1 (2025-06-16):</strong> Integrated Swedish model, detailed financing, support structures.</li> <li><strong>0.0 (2025-06-16):</strong> Initial draft for standalone global framework.</li></ul> <hr> <h2>Preface: Terminology & Intent</h2> <p>This framework employs specific analytical terminology that may appear unconventional in technical economic documents. This is intentional and serves multiple audiences simultaneously.</p> <h3>Dual-Register Language</h3> <p>Throughout this document, you will encounter terms like:</p> <ul><li><strong>“Extractive control patterns”</strong> / <strong>“Three-pattern diagnostic”</strong></li> <li><strong>“Capture-resistant design”</strong> / <strong>“Worth-coercion resistance”</strong> / <strong>“Scarcity-resistance architecture”</strong></li> <li><strong>“Material security compact”</strong> / <strong>“Design intent preservation”</strong></li></ul> <p>These terms derive from Maria Karlova’s structural analysis of recurring power dynamics in economic systems, refined through the GGF’s Accountable Coordination framework (Integrated Meta-Governance Framework §2.1). For readers approaching this as systems architecture, they translate directly to:</p> <table><thead><tr><th>Analytical Term</th><th>Systems Design Translation</th><th>Technical Implication</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Extractive control patterns</strong></td><td><strong>Feedback loops with self-reinforcing lock-in exhibiting four structural features: self-justifying authority, asymmetric transparency, prohibitive exit costs, self-perpetuation as primary function</strong></td><td>Design systems where all four features are structurally absent</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pattern 1: Hierarchy without accountability</strong></td><td><strong>Centralized authority with ideological justification and no revocability mechanism</strong></td><td>Implement distributed governance with transparent algorithms and revocable delegation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pattern 2: Worth-coercion</strong></td><td><strong>Extrinsic motivation via negative social feedback tied to institutional compliance</strong></td><td>Create intrinsic value recognition independent of productivity metrics</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pattern 3: Artificial scarcity</strong></td><td><strong>Supply constraints engineered to enable extraction rather than reflecting genuine resource limits</strong></td><td>Ensure transparent issuance rules and eliminate arbitrary gatekeeping</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Capture-resistant design</strong></td><td><strong>Resilient against institutional capture</strong></td><td>Build protocols that degrade when co-opted rather than serve new masters</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Design intent preservation</strong></td><td><strong>Preservation of design intent across iterations</strong></td><td>Embed principles in architecture, not just documentation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Material security compact</strong></td><td><strong>Baseline security removing survival coercion</strong></td><td>Universal material floor enabling genuine choice</td></tr></tbody></table> <h3>The Accountable Coordination Anchor</h3> <p>The Karlova analysis identifies three recurring extractive patterns. But the GGF itself creates institutions with authority — the Social Resilience Council, the Earth Council veto, the LEKH quorum requirement. Without a principled distinction between the authority structures GGF condemns and the authority structures GGF creates, the framework’s credibility rests on “trust us, we’re different.”</p> <p>Accountable Coordination, as defined in the Integrated Meta-Governance Framework §2.1, provides this distinction through four testable structural criteria. An institution exhibits extractive patterns when it has: (1) self-justifying authority, (2) asymmetric transparency, (3) prohibitive exit costs, and (4) self-perpetuation as primary function. An institution operates as accountable coordination when it has: (1) revocable authority, (2) symmetric transparency, (3) structurally protected exit, and (4) self-limiting function.</p> <p>This distinction is not aspirational — it is testable. Every AUBI governance body is subject to the annual Accountable Coordination Audit (Integrated Meta-Governance Framework, Appendix I), which applies these four criteria and publishes results regardless of findings. The Karlova analysis tells us what patterns to design against; the four-criteria test tells us whether we have succeeded or failed.</p> <h3>Why Both Registers Matter</h3> <p><strong>For the Radical Thinker</strong>: The frank terminology acknowledges that economic systems are not value-neutral. They either concentrate power or distribute it, enable extraction or regeneration, create dependence or autonomy. Refusing to name these dynamics is itself a political choice that serves existing power structures.</p> <p><strong>For the Systems Architect</strong>: The same mechanisms that resist extractive capture are simply good distributed systems design — fault tolerance, Byzantine resistance, incentive alignment, graceful degradation. A system designed to resist co-option is a system designed to remain useful to its stated stakeholders rather than being repurposed by new rent-seekers.</p> <p><strong>For the Policy Maker</strong>: These principles translate to concrete governance requirements: reserve ratios, demurrage rates, validation thresholds, governance multisignatures. The implementation is empirically testable regardless of one’s view on the underlying power analysis.</p> <h3>Scope and Limits of the Karlova Analysis</h3> <p>The three-pattern diagnostic is a useful tool for identifying specific extractive dynamics in economic systems. It is not a totalizing theory of civilization. Human governance history includes both extractive patterns and genuine coordination achievements — sometimes in the same institution simultaneously. The analytical framework identifies what to design against; it does not claim that all prior governance was uniformly parasitic.</p> <p>This scoping matters for two practical reasons:</p> <ol><li><p><strong>Working with people inside legacy institutions.</strong> <em>[Editorial note: this previously cited a “Permeation Mandate” in an earlier Genesis Protocol edition. Genesis v0.9 contains no such mandate, and §1.4 and §6.6 instead constrain what demonstration and engagement may claim. The reasoning below stands on its own; the upstream anchor needs a decision.]</em> The transition depends on people working inside legacy institutions. Framing their entire institutional context as “parasitic” alienates exactly the actors the transition depends on. Framing specific patterns as extractive while acknowledging that the institution also contains genuine coordination capacity is both more accurate and more strategically effective.</p></li> <li><p><strong>Falsifiability</strong>: “All civilization is parasitic” is unfalsifiable and therefore not a design specification. “This institution exhibits Pattern 1 (hierarchy without accountability) because its authority is self-justifying and its exit costs are prohibitive” is testable and actionable. The framework prioritizes the latter.</p></li></ol> <h3>Intellectual Honesty</h3> <p>We maintain this dual-register approach rather than sanitizing the language because:</p> <ol><li><p><strong>Precision</strong>: The analytical terms capture dynamics that softer language obscures. “Systemic fragility” is not the same as deliberately engineered extraction mechanisms.</p></li> <li><p><strong>Resistance to Mission Drift</strong>: If we cannot name what we are designing against, the design will drift toward replicating it. Generations of “alternative” economic projects have been absorbed precisely because they never clearly articulated what they were alternative <em>to</em>.</p></li> <li><p><strong>Education</strong>: Implementers need to understand both what they are building and what will try to subvert it. The Hearts Treasury will face regulatory capture attempts. The Love Ledger will face gamification pressure. These are not hypothetical risks — they are predictable based on historical pattern analysis.</p></li> <li><p><strong>Accountability</strong>: The four-criteria test means the framework can be held to its own standard. If AUBI governance develops self-justifying authority, asymmetric transparency, prohibitive exit costs, or self-perpetuation as primary function, the Karlova analysis applies to AUBI itself. This is a feature, not a bug.</p></li></ol> <h3>How to Read This Document</h3> <ul><li><p><strong>If you are implementing</strong>: Focus on the technical specifications. The analytical language provides design constraints (e.g., “must resist hierarchical capture” → “implement distributed validation with reputation staking”).</p></li> <li><p><strong>If you are governing</strong>: The analytical framework explains <em>why</em> certain seemingly inefficient design choices (like demurrage or validation delays) are actually essential resilience features.</p></li> <li><p><strong>If you are researching</strong>: Both registers are part of the research object. How economic systems resist or enable power concentration is a legitimate empirical question, not merely ideological positioning.</p></li> <li><p><strong>If you are skeptical</strong>: Good. Examine the mechanisms themselves. The mathematics of demurrage, the cryptography of Web of Trust, the economics of AUBI — these are independently verifiable. If they work as designed, the analytical framework’s accuracy can be evaluated by whether the predicted capture attempts occur and whether the defenses hold.</p></li></ul> <h3>Translation is Not Dilution</h3> <p>The companion Love Ledger technical specification (<code>TECHNICAL_SPECIFICATION.md</code>) and README use professional/biomimetic language for strategic reasons — reducing dismissal vectors in institutional contexts. This is translation, not abandonment of analysis.</p> <p>Both registers describe the same architecture. Both are true. Both are necessary.</p> <p>This preface exists so that when you encounter “capture-resistant design” in §2.4, you understand it as both:</p> <ul><li>A claim about power dynamics (systems either concentrate power or distribute it)</li> <li>A boring systems requirement (resistant to institutional capture, testable via four structural criteria)</li></ul> <p>The tension between these readings is productive. It is the design specification.</p> <hr> <h2>1 Executive Summary</h2> <ul><li><strong>Vision:</strong> AUBI is the living expression of the <strong>Treaty for Our Only Home</strong>, channeling <strong>Hearts</strong> and <strong>Leaves</strong> as the lifeblood of a regenerative economy guided by the <strong>Indigenous & Traditional Knowledge Governance Framework</strong>. It ensures material liberation, fosters flourishing societies, and nurtures <strong>Right Relationship</strong> with human and non-human communities, assessed through the <strong>Love, Meaning, and Connection Index (LMCI)</strong>. Contributions are valued and ethically distributed via the Moral Operating System (MOS). The system operates through two parallel mechanisms: 1. A formal, monetized care economy supported by <strong>Hearts</strong> credits, which are earned by chartered <strong>Community Providers</strong> and stabilized by a <strong>Hearts Treasury</strong>. 2. An informal care economy recognized by the <strong>Love Ledger</strong>, which uses non-monetized <strong>Gratitude Tokens</strong> to build social cohesion and provide voluntary qualitative context for population-level interpretation without feeding directly into benefit scoring or being mechanically aggregated into LMCI-M.</li> <li><strong>Approach:</strong> AUBI evolves traditional UBI into a four-layered system, delivering a universal baseline (hybrid fiat/Hearts), adaptive bonuses, equity-based supplements, and recognition of informal contributions, integrated with the <strong>Meta-Governance Framework</strong>, <strong>Financial Systems Framework</strong>, and <strong>Treaty mechanisms</strong>.</li> <li><strong>Goals:</strong> Eliminate poverty, validate care and ecological work, promote cultural and spiritual flourishing, ensure reparative justice, and foster resilient societies in Right Relationship with the planet.</li> <li><strong>Key Features:</strong> <ul><li>Four-tiered payout structure, with Layer 0 as the philosophical foundation honoring relational wealth.</li> <li>Financing via automation taxes, resource levies, Commons Dividends, and Hearts/Leaves, stabilized by Treaty’s Pillar 4.</li> <li>Governance via <strong>Social Resilience Council</strong> under Meta-Governance, with Earth Council veto power.</li> <li>LMCI rooted in Right Relationship, validated against GNH and OECD Better Life Index.</li> <li>Crisis protocols coordinated by Meta-Governance, with Indigenous-led recovery.</li> <li>Cultural adaptation with Indigenous sovereignty, FPIC 2.0, and Red Lines Clause.</li> <li>Comprehensive appendices for failure recovery, political economy, technical architecture, case studies, and observability protocols.</li> <li>Decision observability safeguards requiring explicit uncertainty, alternative hypotheses, challenge rights, and outcome validation before consequential interventions.</li></ul></li></ul> <p><strong>Ecosystem Integration Note:</strong> This framework does not operate in isolation. Key vulnerabilities identified by external review—elite exit, transition shock, geopolitical resistance, and long-term drift—are addressed through deep integration with the broader GGF ecosystem. Readers evaluating AUBI as a standalone document should note that critical immune responses are distributed across the <strong>Treaty for Our Only Home</strong> (geopolitical strategy, global taxation), <strong>Work in Liberation Framework</strong> (labor transition, industrial sunsetting), <strong>Genesis Protocol</strong> (drift detection, backlash mitigation), and <strong>Financial Systems Framework</strong> (sovereign debt transformation, reserve stability). Cross-references throughout this document indicate where these architectural defenses reside.</p> <ul><li><strong>Risks:</strong> Gaming, digital divide, capital flight, cultural conflicts, false identifiability, exclusion of non-participants, correlated observation failures, and metric self-validation, mitigated through Indigenous-guided governance, independent observation channels, community validation, diagnostic restraint, and stress testing protocols.</li> <li><strong>Readiness:</strong> Ready for bounded pilot development in Indigenous-led Bioregional Autonomous Zones (BAZs). Full BAZ formalization and consequential adaptive allocation are conditional on the observability, appeal, and administrative-capacity safeguards specified in §4.5, §7.4, §8, and Appendix G.</li> <li><strong>Capture-Resistant Design:</strong> Built with structural resistance to the three extractive patterns identified in the Karlova analysis, testable via the four-criteria Accountable Coordination framework</li> <li><strong>Simultaneous Resistance:</strong> Addresses all three extractive patterns concurrently rather than sequentially, preventing adaptive capture</li> <li><strong>Design Intent Preservation:</strong> Transmission mechanisms that preserve capture-resistant principles across institutional iterations</li></ul> <p><strong>Predicted Capture Attempts:</strong> Institutions exhibiting extractive patterns will actively work to co-opt or neutralize AUBI through:</p> <ul><li><strong>Worth-Coercion Narratives:</strong> ‘Lazy welfare recipients’ framing designed to reimpose compliance-based worth measurement</li> <li><strong>Scarcity Panic:</strong> ‘There’s not enough’ despite evidence, designed to reimpose artificial supply constraints</li> <li><strong>Hierarchical Capture:</strong> Attempts to recentralize distribution authority in ways that satisfy self-justifying authority criteria</li> <li><strong>Semantic Inversion:</strong> Turning liberation language into control mechanisms (e.g., “empowerment” meaning “self-exploitation”)</li></ul> <p><strong>Mitigation:</strong> Capture-resistant architecture, community-based validation, annual Accountable Coordination Audit, and rapid response to co-option attempts</p> <p>AUBI is designed with structural resistance to three recurring extractive patterns identified in the Karlova analysis:</p> <ul><li><strong>Pattern 3 (Artificial Scarcity):</strong> Unconditional material security eliminates survival anxiety as a compliance mechanism, breaking the linkage between institutional obedience and basic needs</li> <li><strong>Pattern 2 (Worth-Coercion):</strong> Validates care work and cultural contributions as genuinely valuable, creating recognition pathways independent of market-productivity metrics</li> <li><strong>Pattern 1 (Hierarchy without Accountability):</strong> Distributes economic decision-making power to individuals and communities through revocable, transparent, exit-protected governance structures</li></ul> <hr> <h2>2 Introduction: AUBI as the Heart of a Regenerative Civilization</h2> <h3>2.1 A Foundation for Right Relationship</h3> <p>Guided by the <strong>Indigenous & Traditional Knowledge Governance Framework</strong>, AUBI is the economic engine for the Treaty’s liberation compact, ensuring material liberation while nurturing <strong>Right Relationship</strong>—the interconnected web of human, non-human, and planetary life. It unlocks potential for innovation, community flourishing, and cultural revitalization, aligned with the Meaningful Existence as a Social Right framework. The system operates through two parallel mechanisms: 1. A formal, monetized care economy supported by <strong>Hearts</strong> credits, which are earned by chartered <strong>Community Providers</strong> and stabilized by a <strong>Hearts Treasury</strong>. 2. An informal care economy recognized by the <strong>Love Ledger</strong>, which uses non-monetized <strong>Gratitude Tokens</strong> to build social cohesion and inform the <strong>LMCI</strong> metric.</p> <p><strong>Right Relationship Defined:</strong></p> <ul><li><strong>Material liberation:</strong> Freedom from survival anxiety via a hybrid fiat/Hearts baseline.</li> <li><strong>Relational Wealth:</strong> Prioritizing kinship, community, and ecological harmony (Layer 0).</li> <li><strong>Restorative Justice:</strong> Reparations for historical and ecological harms (Layer 3).</li> <li><strong>Planetary Flourishing:</strong> Contributions to care, culture, and ecology, rewarded by Hearts/Leaves.</li></ul> <p><strong>Historical Inspirations:</strong> Indigenous gift economies, Alaska Permanent Fund, Brazil’s Bolsa FamÃ­lia, Finland’s UBI pilot, Kenya’s GiveDirectly, reimagined through Indigenous wisdom for global scalability.</p> <h3>2.2 AUBI vs. Traditional UBI</h3> <p>AUBI is a living system for enacting Treaty promises, channeling Hearts/Leaves in parallel with the non-monetized Love Ledger, guided by Indigenous principles.</p> <table><thead><tr><th><strong>Feature</strong></th><th><strong>Traditional UBI</strong></th><th><strong>Adaptive UBI (AUBI)</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Philosophy</strong></td><td>Welfare supplement</td><td>Expression of Right Relationship and Treaty liberation compact</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Payout</strong></td><td>Fixed fiat</td><td>Hybrid fiat/Hearts baseline, dynamic bonuses, Leaves for ecology</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Conditionality</strong></td><td>Unconditional</td><td>Unconditional baseline + voluntary contributions for Hearts/Leaves</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metrics</strong></td><td>Scarcity dismantling</td><td>LMCI (Right Relationship, care, ecology, culture)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Governance</strong></td><td>State-based</td><td>Social Resilience Council under Meta-Governance, Earth Council veto</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cultural Approach</strong></td><td>Universal</td><td>Indigenous-guided, community-adapted with FPIC 2.0</td></tr></tbody></table> <h3>2.3 The Guiding Compass: Love, Meaning, and Connection Index (LMCI)</h3> <p>LMCI measures collective flourishing through <strong>Right Relationship</strong>, providing a population-level governance signal that complements and eventually displaces GDP as the primary indicator of civilizational health. It is validated against GNH and the OECD Better Life Index.</p> <p><strong>Four information products must remain architecturally distinct:</strong></p> <p><strong>LMCI-Measurement (LMCI-M)</strong> is a population-level system-health estimate. It aggregates independent population surveys, longitudinal studies, ecological monitoring, cultural-vitality assessments, and Indigenous knowledge-holder observations. It is specifically <em>not</em> derived from individual AUBI contribution claims, provider activity totals, Hearts transaction volume, or Love Ledger recognition counts. LMCI-M feeds the Meta-Governance Reflexivity Engine and the SRC’s policy review process through the multi-timescale architecture in §8.</p> <p><strong>LMCI-Allocation (LMCI-A)</strong> is the per-contribution scoring system that governs AUBI Layer 2 bonuses. It operates at the level of chartered Community Provider claims, validated through the Proof of Care protocol. LMCI-A uses the same Love/Meaning/Connection vocabulary as LMCI-M but draws from a separate data pipeline: provider activity logs, recipient acceptance where appropriate, Community Weaver attestations, ecological evidence, and Hearts Treasury records.</p> <p><strong>Operational Telemetry</strong> records the functioning of the AUBI and Hearts systems: circulation, redemption, reserves, provider capacity, waiting times, failed transactions, administrative backlogs, enrolment, access, and fraud alerts. Operational telemetry may trigger diagnostic review but is <strong>not LMCI-M</strong> and must not be presented as evidence of flourishing merely because activity increases.</p> <p><strong>The Love Ledger Narrative Feed</strong> contains voluntary, non-monetized records and community narratives concerning informal care, gratitude, reciprocity, and relational life. It may generate hypotheses, contextualize LMCI-M findings, and identify support needs, but it is not mechanically converted into LMCI-M, LMCI-A, Hearts, fiat, or individual entitlement.</p> <p><strong>Why the separation is non-negotiable:</strong> If contribution claims, transaction activity, or recognition counts fed directly into LMCI-M, the measurement signal would track incentivized or recorded behavior rather than genuine flourishing—the same category error that causes GDP to measure economic activity rather than human welfare. A community gaming AUBI bonuses may distort LMCI-A and trigger fraud review, but it must not thereby manufacture an improvement in LMCI-M. Likewise, a policy that raises Hearts velocity has not demonstrated improved human or ecological conditions unless independent outcome channels also improve.</p> <p><strong>LMCI-M Components</strong> (population-level, independent sources):</p> <ul><li><strong>Love:</strong> Stratified wellbeing surveys, social cohesion indicators, care-relationship quality assessments, and sampled relationship-network measures—never simple totals of logged care acts.</li> <li><strong>Meaning:</strong> Cultural vitality assessments by Indigenous and community councils, longitudinal tracking of meaningful engagement, knowledge transmission, and voluntary purpose-seeking.</li> <li><strong>Connection:</strong> Ecological monitoring, sacred-site health indicators, biodiversity measures, and long-embedded community environmental observations—co-interpreted with relevant knowledge holders under the Slow-Variable Decision Protocol (§4.3).</li></ul> <p><strong>LMCI-A Components</strong> (per-contribution, provider-level):</p> <ul><li><strong>Love:</strong> Verified care, mentorship, and conflict-resolution contributions, with evidence appropriate to the claim being made.</li> <li><strong>Meaning:</strong> Cultural preservation, knowledge sharing, and spiritual-care delivery validated through culturally legitimate processes.</li> <li><strong>Connection:</strong> Ecological restoration and land stewardship supported by the Green Job Score and an ecological evidence bundle where outcome claims are made.</li></ul> <p><strong>No Individual LMCI-M Score:</strong> LMCI-M is never assigned to an individual and cannot be made portable as an identity, eligibility, migration, or entitlement score. Portable AUBI rights use claimant-controlled <strong>purpose-limited access, entitlement, and continuity records</strong> under §5.1.1 and §5.3, subject to CIS v0.2.4 and Aurora v1.3.2. Portability of an AUBI claim does not create portability of general identity.</p> <p><strong>Transition Strategy:</strong> LMCI-M complements GDP for an initial five-year transition period while its validity, cultural calibration, subgroup behavior, and policy usefulness are tested. LMCI-A may operate from pilot launch only after Community Weaver validation, claimant challenge rights, and the Proof of Care safeguards in §5.2 are functioning.</p> <h3>2.4 AUBI as structural resistance to extractive patterns</h3> <p>The Karlova analysis identifies three recurring extractive patterns in economic systems. These are not unique to any era or culture — they are emergent properties of centralized coordination under conditions of information asymmetry and survival dependency. AUBI is designed with structural resistance to all three:</p> <p><strong>Structural countermeasures to each pattern:</strong></p> <ol><li><p><strong>Against Artificial Scarcity (Pattern 3):</strong></p> <ul><li>Unconditional baseline security eliminates survival anxiety as compliance mechanism</li> <li>Breaks the linkage between debt obligation and institutional obedience</li> <li>Demonstrates through operation that resources exist for baseline security when not artificially constrained</li> <li>Creates economic space for genuine choice rather than coerced compliance</li></ul></li> <li><p><strong>Against Worth-Coercion (Pattern 2):</strong></p> <ul><li>Validates care, creativity, and community work as genuinely valuable through Hearts/Love Ledger recognition</li> <li>Creates recognition pathways independent of market-productivity metrics</li> <li>Separates human worth from institutional performance evaluation</li> <li>Ends the compliance-linkage that makes self-worth contingent on employer approval</li></ul></li> <li><p><strong>Against Hierarchy without Accountability (Pattern 1):</strong></p> <ul><li>Distributes economic decision-making power to individuals and communities</li> <li>All AUBI governance bodies satisfy the four-criteria Accountable Coordination test (revocable authority, symmetric transparency, protected exit, self-limiting function)</li> <li>Creates fluid authority structures where power must be continuously earned through demonstrated coordination value</li> <li>Replaces abstract ownership claims with stewardship relationships subject to community oversight</li></ul></li></ol> <p><strong>Capture-resistant design principles:</strong></p> <ul><li><strong>Resistance to Co-option:</strong> Hearts/Love Ledger architecture degrades rather than serves new masters when captured — demurrage, distributed validation, and community chartering prevent any single actor from repurposing the system for extraction</li> <li><strong>Design Intent Preservation:</strong> Capture-resistant principles embedded in architecture (reserve ratios, multi-signature governance, BAZ reformation triggers), not just documentation</li> <li><strong>Simultaneous Resistance:</strong> All three patterns addressed concurrently to prevent adaptive capture — addressing only one pattern allows the others to compensate</li> <li><strong>Self-Testing:</strong> Annual Accountable Coordination Audit applies the four-criteria test to AUBI’s own governance, ensuring the framework holds itself to its own standard</li></ul> <hr> <h2>3 Treaty Integration: AUBI as the Liberation compact’s Delivery Mechanism</h2> <p>AUBI delivers the Treaty’s social promises, integrated across all pillars:</p> <table><thead><tr><th><strong>AUBI Component</strong></th><th><strong>Treaty Pillar</strong></th><th><strong>Mechanisms</strong></th><th><strong>Crisis Integration</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Financing</strong></td><td>P4 (Planetary Health)</td><td>Carbon taxes, Leaves for restoration, Hearts stabilization</td><td>Emergency funding via P4</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Verification</strong></td><td>P2 (Global Forensic Corps)</td><td>Love Ledger validation, fraud prevention</td><td>Crisis evidence analysis</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Implementation</strong></td><td>P1 (Reform Accelerator Labs)</td><td>Pilot testing in BAZs, legislative templates</td><td>Rapid policy adaptation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ethics & Culture</strong></td><td>P5 (Ethical Infrastructure)</td><td>Indigenous-guided adaptation, FPIC 2.0</td><td>Values preservation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Governance</strong></td><td>P3 (Humanitarian Stability)</td><td>Social Resilience Council, Earth Council veto</td><td>Multi-region crisis coordination</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Adversarial Testing</strong></td><td>P5 (Office of the Adversary)</td><td>Cultural conflict simulations, elite capture prevention</td><td>System stress testing</td></tr></tbody></table> <hr> <h2>4 Governance Model: Social Resilience Council under Meta-Governance</h2> <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Ensures equitable, transparent, and Indigenous-guided oversight, with AUBI as a core tool of the <strong>Meta-Governance Framework</strong>.</p> <h3>4.1 Social Resilience Council (Meta-Governance Coordination Council)</h3> <ul><li><strong>Mandate:</strong> Oversee fiscal health, risk management, and equitable distribution of AUBI payouts, implementing standards set by the Fractal Labor Parliament (FLP).</li> <li><strong>Role:</strong> Primary council for socio-economic flourishing, operating under Treaty’s P3 and Meta-Governance, guided by Earth Council’s moral authority.</li></ul> <h4>Representation requirements and composition status</h4> <p>The Social Resilience Council’s actual composition and selection architecture shall be established by its <strong>own valid constituting charter</strong> under Integrated Meta-Governance v1.6.1 §3.1 and §3.7.</p> <p>AUBI identifies the following <strong>representation requirements</strong> because they are material to the council’s function:</p> <ul><li>people and communities materially dependent on or affected by AUBI administration, including low-income and low-documentation participants;</li> <li>affected Indigenous peoples and authorities where their rights, territories, economies, knowledge, or institutions are implicated, through representatives or other interfaces chosen under those peoples’ own processes;</li> <li>youth and long-horizon perspectives where decisions materially shape intergenerational conditions;</li> <li>scientific, social-economic, implementation, and public-finance expertise relevant to AUBI;</li> <li>anti-capture, accountability, labour, disability, care, and community perspectives sufficient to test whether administration is reproducing exclusion or concentrated power.</li></ul> <p>These requirements do <strong>not</strong> prescribe a universal percentage formula. The former 40/30/20/10 profile is retired as a binding rule and may be retained only as historical design material. In particular:</p> <ul><li>no universal Indigenous seat percentage is created;</li> <li>no GGF body ratifies who represents an Indigenous people unless that people has separately authorized that role;</li> <li>youth participation need not use one global age band, electorate, or BAZ-cluster formula;</li> <li>expertise seats are expertise functions, not democratic constituencies;</li> <li>regulated corporations or entities with direct Hearts-economy interests receive no automatic representational entitlement.</li></ul> <h4>Selection, terms, vacancies, and mandate</h4> <p>Selection method, eligibility, term length, renewal limits, staggered turnover, vacancy replacement, recall/removal, conflict rules, and selection challenges shall be defined by the SRC’s valid constituting charter.</p> <p>Earlier AUBI defaults such as two-year staggered terms, 50% maximum turnover, or a 90-day vacancy target may be considered as <strong>illustrative continuity options</strong>. They are not self-executing constitutional rules.</p> <p>No Earth Council, Meta-Governance body, AUBI administrator, or framework author may fill a vacancy merely because the chartered selector has not acted. Any interim appointment requires authority from the constituting instrument.</p> <blockquote><p><strong>Selection determines who may occupy an SRC office. It does not expand the SRC’s jurisdiction. Representation is not consent, FPIC, Treaty accession, or authority over non-constituents.</strong></p></blockquote> <h4>Conflict-of-Interest Protocol</h4> <ul><li>Members must disclose all financial relationships with entities operating Hearts-denominated services, BAZ councils with pending SRC decisions, or technology providers to the Love Ledger/Hearts Treasury.</li> <li>Disclosed conflicts require recusal from the specific decision; undisclosed conflicts discovered post-decision trigger automatic referral to the Digital Justice Tribunal.</li> <li>No member may accept employment, advisory roles, or Hearts-denominated grants from entities regulated by SRC decisions for 18 months after leaving the council.</li></ul> <h4>Capture-Prevention Architecture</h4> <ul><li>Hearts-economy transaction volume shall not itself purchase, weight, or entitle representation. Any lawful seat or voting allocation must be justified by the SRC charter’s stated representation function rather than economic throughput.</li> <li>Any proposal that would concentrate Hearts issuance authority, remove demurrage, or create tiered redemption access requires the heightened decision rule enacted by the valid SRC charter and any separately required Treaty, Financial Systems, Indigenous, or rights authority. A generic supermajority of “Indigenous seats” does not substitute for affected-peoples’ consent where consent is actually required.</li> <li>An annual independent audit assesses whether SRC composition, agenda access, and decisions systematically favor high-Hearts-volume BAZs, funders, vendors, or other concentrated interests. The auditor’s appointment and independence must follow the charter; Meta-Governance does not appoint the auditor by default.</li></ul> <h4>Functions</h4> <ul><li>Oversee AUBI implementation, Hearts/Leaves distribution, LMCI-M, LMCI-A, and operational telemetry as distinct systems with separate purposes and validation chains.</li> <li>Administer Global Commons Fund via Treaty P4.</li> <li>Mediate disputes via Digital Justice Tribunal, with Earth Council veto over ecological/cultural violations.</li> <li>Adjust the fiat/Hearts ratio only after the Material Security Estimate and Hearts Usability & Freedom Test (§5.1.2), with subgroup analysis, a published State Estimate Record, and Slow-Variable Decision Protocol review (§4.3) where long-term dependency trajectories are affected.</li> <li><strong>Accountable Coordination Audit:</strong> All SRC functions are subject to the annual <strong>Accountable Coordination Audit</strong>. This audit applies four testable criteria—revocable authority, symmetric transparency, protected exit, and self-limiting function—and publishes results regardless of findings. The audit protocol is defined in the Integrated Meta-Governance Framework, Appendix I.</li></ul> <h4>Earth Council Veto</h4> <p>Halts AUBI plans in BAZs threatening ecological integrity or cultural sovereignty.</p> <hr> <h3>4.2 Operationalization</h3> <ul><li><strong>Crisis Protocols:</strong> Triggered by Meta-Governance (e.g., inflation >10%, ecological crises), with Indigenous-led recovery. Emergency authority is time-limited to 90 days with mandatory full-council review before any extension.</li> <li><strong>Slow-Variable Quorum Requirement:</strong> Any decision qualifying under the Slow-Variable Decision Category (SVDC) requires affirmative sign-off from a Long-Embedded Knowledge Holder (LEKH) quorum before proceeding. See §4.3 for full definitions, qualifying decision list, and quorum mechanics.</li> <li><strong>Multi-Timescale Reflexivity Feed:</strong> Operational pulses and quarterly nowcasts may provide provisional warning, while the annual LMCI-M estimate and Slow-Variable Register provide the primary population and long-horizon evidence. These products, LMCI-A, and operational telemetry remain methodologically independent and visibly labelled. See §2.3 and §8.</li> <li><strong>Decision Observability:</strong> Consequential decisions must follow the Decision Observability and Epistemic Governance Protocol (§4.5), including admissible evidence, alternative hypotheses, uncertainty, challenge rights, and outcome reassessment.</li> <li><strong>Coordination:</strong> Regional Steward Councils and Value Courts operate under Social Resilience Council, with BAZs as local hubs.</li> <li><strong>Structural Review Petition:</strong> The SRC charter should provide a practical route by which participating BAZs and affected constituencies can trigger independent structural review. The former 30%-within-12-months threshold and 180-day timetable are illustrative design options unless enacted by the valid charter. Meta-Governance may support or route the review but does not make its findings binding by framework declaration. The NARP objection pathway (Financial Systems §12) remains the first-line mechanism for specific ICTL changes.</li></ul> <h3>4.3 Slow-Variable Decision Protocol</h3> <h4>Rationale</h4> <p>Fast economic and political signals (Hearts velocity, quarterly LMCI-A scores, inflation indicators) are structurally louder than slow ecological signals (permafrost thaw trajectories, migratory route shifts, decadal soil health trends, inter-generational fishery dynamics). In any mixed council operating under time pressure, fast signals will dominate deliberation unless slow-variable categories are explicitly ring-fenced. The Earth Council veto (§4.1) is a reactive mechanism — it blocks identifiable harm. This section establishes a complementary proactive mechanism: certain decision categories cannot proceed without affirmative sign-off from long-embedded knowledge holders, regardless of the broader council’s disposition.</p> <h4>Slow-Variable Decision Category (SVDC)</h4> <p>A decision qualifies as SVDC if it materially affects any variable whose consequences manifest primarily on a timescale of 10 years or longer, and where the primary observational record is held by communities with continuous, multigenerational presence in affected ecosystems. Specific qualifying decisions include, but are not limited to:</p> <ul><li>Leaves valuation formulas and ecological multipliers (Green Job Score baselines)</li> <li>Carbon sequestration thresholds used in Leaves issuance</li> <li>AUBI issuance caps tied to ecological regeneration benchmarks</li> <li>Industrial Sunsetting timelines for extractive sectors</li> <li>Hearts Treasury reserve ratio adjustments that alter long-term fiat dependency trajectories</li> <li>Any revision to LMCI-M ecological components (§2.3) that changes which slow signals feed governance dashboards</li></ul> <p>Economic decisions with purely short-cycle consequences (quarterly Hearts supply adjustments within established bands, individual BAZ dispute resolutions, administrative fee structures) do not qualify as SVDC.</p> <p>When classification is disputed, the Earth Council makes the determination, with appeals to the Digital Justice Tribunal.</p> <h4>Long-Embedded Knowledge Holder (LEKH) Quorum</h4> <p>Not all Indigenous SRC seats carry LEKH quorum rights for all decisions. LEKH quorum rights attach to <em>communities</em>, not to individuals, based on demonstrated multigenerational observational presence in the specific ecosystem affected by a given SVDC. Criteria:</p> <ul><li>The community holds verified traditional ecological knowledge records (oral, written, or material) spanning a minimum of three generations for the relevant ecosystem type</li> <li>The community’s territory or primary subsistence practice is directly affected by the decision’s projected slow-variable consequences</li> <li>The community’s SRC delegate is formally nominated by that community’s governance body (not merely Indigenous by heritage)</li></ul> <p>For each SVDC, the Earth Council identifies which communities hold LEKH quorum rights for that specific decision, drawing from a registry maintained by the Indigenous & Traditional Knowledge Governance Framework. A community may hold LEKH rights for ocean decisions but not forest decisions; specificity matters.</p> <p><strong>Quorum threshold:</strong> A SVDC requires affirmative votes from a minimum of 60% of LEKH-qualified delegates identified for that decision. Abstention does not count as affirmative. If fewer than three LEKH-qualified delegates are available for a decision, the decision is automatically deferred until adequate representation is secured (maximum deferral: 90 days, with a mandatory status report at 45 days).</p> <p><strong>Separation from veto:</strong> LEKH quorum is a positive requirement, not a veto. A SVDC that receives 60%+ LEKH affirmative votes can still be blocked by the Earth Council veto if it threatens ecological integrity — the two mechanisms are independent and cumulative.</p> <h3>4.4 Long-Term Identity Drift Prevention</h3> <p>Systems do not only fail through capture; they also fail through <strong>slow semantic drift</strong>—“care credits” becoming bureaucratic KPIs, the “Love Ledger” becoming a surveillance tool, or “liberation” language being inverted into new forms of control. The AUBI governance architecture includes institutional immune responses distributed across the GGF ecosystem to detect and correct such drift:</p> <table><thead><tr><th><strong>Drift Detection Mechanism</strong></th><th><strong>Description</strong></th><th><strong>GGF Framework</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Founding Intent Record and mandate review</strong></td><td>Every founding or transitional body records its initial purpose, scope, prohibited powers, assumptions, dependencies, red lines, and dissolution conditions. Periodic review compares original mandate against current behaviour and dependency. Divergence opens a review; it does not by itself authorize intervention.</td><td>Genesis Protocol §16.4–16.5</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Governance Integrity System</strong></td><td>Includes <strong>System Sanity Audits</strong> and <strong>Cognitive Friction Audits</strong> to detect procedural absurdity, metric gaming, and “economization of the soul.”</td><td>Meta-Governance Framework, Appendix E</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Council of Guardians</strong></td><td>A body of 9–12 long-term thinkers serving single 15-year terms, mandated to conduct <strong>Principle Impact Reviews</strong> and ensure intergenerational alignment. No executive power, but can force supermajority reconsideration votes.</td><td>Treaty for Our Only Home, Pillar 1</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Office of the Adversary</strong></td><td>Independent agency staffed with red team specialists and adversarial journalists, mandated to challenge proposals and publish dissenting reports alongside major policies.</td><td>Treaty for Our Only Home, Pillar 5</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reformation Trigger (30% BAZ Petition)</strong></td><td>If 30% or more of active BAZs formally petition for structural review within 12 months, an independent constitutional review is automatically initiated.</td><td>§4.2</td></tr></tbody></table> <p><strong>Note on Cultural Renewal:</strong> The mechanisms listed above are procedural safeguards. They detect and correct drift, but they do not <em>generate</em> cultural vitality. The GGF ecosystem includes complementary infrastructure for symbolic, emotional, and identity-forming renewal—<strong>Global Meaning Infrastructure</strong> (Treaty Pillar 5), <strong>Cultural Heritage Framework</strong>, and the <strong>Arts Flourishing Framework</strong>. Effectiveness of these cultural layers depends on ongoing community ownership and cannot be reduced to technical enforcement. This asymmetry—procedural mechanisms are easier to operationalize than cultural ones—is an acknowledged design tension (see §12.1).</p> <p>These mechanisms ensure that the AUBI framework is not merely designed once and left to drift. It is a <strong>self-correcting governance organism</strong> with built-in sensors and immune responses for long-term fidelity to its founding principles.</p> <h3>4.5 Decision Observability and Epistemic Governance</h3> <p>Adaptive governance requires more than collecting data. It requires an explicit account of what condition a decision is trying to infer, what observations support that inference, what materially different conditions remain compatible with the evidence, and who may challenge the interpretation.</p> <p>Three constitutional rules govern all consequential AUBI decisions:</p> <blockquote><p><strong>An indicator is an alert signal, not a diagnosis.</strong></p></blockquote> <blockquote><p><strong>No uniform intervention may be selected where materially different latent states remain observationally compatible with the available evidence and require conflicting responses.</strong></p></blockquote> <blockquote><p><strong>An intervention’s effect on the indicator that justified it does not constitute evidence that the underlying human or ecological condition improved.</strong></p></blockquote> <h4>4.5.1 Consequential Decision Classes</h4> <p>The protocol applies at minimum to:</p> <ul><li>Layer 1 amount and fiat/Hearts composition;</li> <li>Hearts issuance, demurrage, reserve, and redemption changes;</li> <li>Layer 2 validation, scoring, caps, and fraud findings;</li> <li>Layer 3 needs, equity, reparations, and negative adjustments;</li> <li>ecological multipliers and Leaves issuance;</li> <li>BAZ stage progression or remediation;</li> <li>crisis redirection of work or funds;</li> <li>institutional review, devolution, or adverse action against a participant or provider.</li></ul> <h4>4.5.2 Observation Contracts and State Estimate Records</h4> <p>Each consequential decision class must have a published <strong>Observation Contract</strong> specifying the latent state, admissible channels, observer and data owner, collection method, cadence, latency, known missingness, source dependencies, estimator or interpretive procedure, alternative hypotheses, uncertainty representation, challenge rights, action range, outcome measure, and protocol version. The reusable template is provided in Appendix G.1.</p> <p>Each individual decision or policy adjustment must produce a <strong>State Estimate Record</strong> stating:</p> <ul><li>the estimated condition and as-of date;</li> <li>evidence supporting and contradicting the estimate;</li> <li>viable alternative explanations;</li> <li>uncertainty, missingness, and source disagreement;</li> <li>affected subgroups and the account supplied by affected parties;</li> <li>the selected action and why it remains acceptable under uncertainty;</li> <li>expected outcomes, reassessment date, and reversal conditions.</li></ul> <p>Where the evidence cannot distinguish states requiring different actions, the official conclusion is:</p> <blockquote><p><strong>Cause unresolved—additional diagnosis required.</strong></p></blockquote> <p>Diagnostic restraint is a valid governance outcome and must not be treated as administrative failure.</p> <h4>4.5.3 Joint States, Sequencing, and Outcome Validation</h4> <p>A BAZ may contain several simultaneous causes, including different conditions in different subgroups. Aggregate averages must not erase these mixtures. Where interventions have prerequisites or interact non-additively, the State Estimate Record must specify sequencing. Reversible diagnostic probes are preferred over irreversible system-wide changes when uncertainty is material.</p> <p>Outcome review must observe the human, social, distributive, and ecological condition the action was intended to improve—not merely the administrative or monetary indicator directly affected by the action. Pre-intervention evidence must be preserved so that self-validating feedback cannot erase the original diagnosis.</p> <h4>4.5.4 Observation Independence and Sensor-Change Governance</h4> <p>Nominally distinct channels are not treated as independent when they share the same observer, platform, funding body, algorithm, incentive, or governance authority. Each BAZ and the SRC maintain an <strong>Observation Independence Map</strong> identifying these dependencies.</p> <p>Changes to observation architecture are governance changes, not neutral technical maintenance. Changing an LMCI instrument, sampling frame, fraud model, missing-data rule, knowledge-holder definition, dashboard aggregation, or balance between human and algorithmic validation requires public versioning. Material changes follow the Notice and Adaptive Response Protocol; changes affecting slow ecological evidence also require SVDC review.</p> <h4>4.5.5 Individual Epistemic Rights</h4> <p>Any person affected by an AUBI access-attestation, person-continuity, eligibility, allocation, contribution, fraud, negative-adjustment, or access decision has the right to:</p> <ol><li>know that a decision is being made;</li> <li>inspect the evidence, model output, and protocol version used;</li> <li>correct factual errors and submit contextual evidence;</li> <li>challenge algorithmic or human interpretation;</li> <li>receive a plain-language rationale;</li> <li>obtain timely human review and appeal;</li> <li>know the reassessment and reversal conditions;</li> <li>exercise data sovereignty and request correction or deletion where compatible with necessary audit obligations.</li></ol> <p>These rights require accessible offline pathways and may not depend on staking Hearts, legal representation, or advanced digital access. Appendix G.3 provides the implementation specification.</p> <hr> <h2>5 AUBI Payout Architecture: Indigenous-Guided Layers</h2> <h3>5.0 Layer 0: Relational Wealth (Philosophical Foundation)</h3> <ul><li><strong>Purpose:</strong> Honors unmonetized contributions (kinship, cultural wisdom, ecological care) as the foundation of Right Relationship.</li> <li><strong>Operationalization:</strong> <ul><li>Documented via Love Ledger with non-monetized <strong>Gratitude Tokens</strong>. The Love Ledger serves as a decentralized, community-validated registry that records voluntary acts of care, gratitude, and mutual support without assigning monetary value. Its Narrative Feed may contextualize population-level inquiry but is not mechanically aggregated into LMCI-M or LMCI-A.</li> <li>Ceremonial recognition in community gatherings.</li> <li>Transition pathways to Layer 2 via mentorship programs.</li></ul></li> <li><strong>Cultural Integration:</strong> Aligns with Indigenous gift economies, Ubuntu, and spiritual traditions.</li></ul> <p><strong>The Sacredness Boundary:</strong> A recognized tension in any framework that measures care is the risk of “economizing the soul”—slowly converting all value into what is measurable and tradeable. The AUBI architecture explicitly contains this tension through <strong>constitutional firewalls</strong>:</p> <ul><li><strong>Layer 0 is non-monetized by design.</strong> <em>Gratitude Tokens</em> in the Love Ledger are <strong>not convertible</strong> to <em>Hearts</em>, fiat, or any Layer 1–3 benefit. They are a social signal, not an economic asset.</li> <li><strong>The Love Ledger and Hearts Treasury are architecturally separate systems</strong> with different validation chains and governance. This separation prevents the gift economy from being absorbed into the exchange economy.</li> <li><strong>Conscience, culture, and voluntary association</strong> (Genesis Protocol §1.5) explicitly limits GGF authority over personal conscience, cultural values, spiritual sovereignty, and family structures. The GGF governs the coordination of shared physical commons <em>only</em>.</li></ul> <p>This is not a claim that the tension between measurement and sacredness is “solved.” It is an acknowledgment that the tension is <strong>irreducible</strong> and must be <strong>contained through structural humility</strong> rather than eliminated through better metrics. Layer 0 exists to remind the system of what it cannot—and should not—measure. A separate, voluntary <strong>Informal Care Burden Sentinel</strong> (Appendix G.5) may identify exhaustion, coercion, or material need without valuing, ranking, or converting the relational act itself.</p> <h3>5.1 Layer 1: Universal Baseline (Hybrid Fiat/Hearts)</h3> <ul><li><strong>Purpose:</strong> Ensures dignity with a hybrid baseline for market and care economies. Universal Baseline as <strong>Material Security Foundation</strong>—ensuring no human being can be controlled through survival anxiety. This removes the structural precondition for Pattern 3 (artificial scarcity as compliance mechanism).</li> <li><strong>Default Composition:</strong> 70% fiat and 30% Hearts at launch, subject to the safeguards in §5.1.2 rather than treated as a permanent universal ratio.</li> <li><strong>Specifications:</strong> <ul><li>Covers all adults 18+, with education stipends and optional youth civic internships (ages 16–18) for early contributions.</li> <li>Initial regional calculation uses price indices covering housing (40%), food (25%), utilities (15%), transport (10%), and healthcare (10%), then tests whether those prices correspond to actual access, availability, and quality.</li> <li>Where the housing component is rising faster than other components in a market receiving Layer 1 at scale, recalculation should be accompanied by the Housing capitalization observation rather than treated as ordinary price movement. A baseline indexed to housing cost can partly chase a rent increase it contributed to.</li> <li>BAZ-specific composition may vary only through the published decision process in §4.5.</li> <li>Layer 1 is disbursed at regular short intervals — monthly or more frequently — rather than as an infrequent lump sum. Cadence is a design parameter, not an administrative detail: a large infrequent payment concentrates consumption, conflict, and predatory attention around the payment date, and widens the gap that any single missed or delayed payment creates.</li></ul></li> <li><strong>Safeguards:</strong> Inflation monitoring, Material Security Estimates, Hearts usability audits, subgroup analysis, Treaty arbitration, individual correction and appeal rights, the Housing capitalization observation (Housing §10.1) where Layer 1 is deployed at scale in a housing market, and the reduction, interruption, and exit procedure in §5.1.3.</li></ul> <h4>5.1.1 Universal Baseline for Stateless, Displaced, and Nomadic Peoples</h4> <p><strong>Principle:</strong> The right to material liberation is inherent to personhood, not contingent on citizenship, fixed address, or possession of conventional identity documents.</p> <p>AUBI follows the CIS v0.2.4 rule:</p> <blockquote><p><strong>Recognition is not registration. Linkage is not identity. Attestation is not jurisdiction.</strong></p></blockquote> <p><strong>Eligibility & Enrollment:</strong></p> <ul><li><strong>Community Attestation:</strong> Individuals without state-issued documentation may supply self-attestation, community attestation, witness evidence, prior records, service-provider evidence, or other proportionate evidence accepted by the competent AUBI enrollment process. A multi-witness model may strengthen an AUBI claim, but it does <strong>not</strong> create general legal identity, citizenship, nationality, migration status, family status, voting eligibility, or legal personhood across the GGF.</li> <li><strong>AUBI Access Attestation:</strong> For a person who cannot reliably use conventional identity infrastructure, the competent AUBI process may issue a lightweight, claimant-controlled <strong>AUBI Access Attestation</strong>. It exists only to maintain continuity of AUBI enrollment, entitlement, and payout access. It may be supported by non-biometric hardware, community-held credentials, claimant-held credentials, or biometrics only where separately lawful, strictly necessary for the chosen implementation, and based on explicit consent with a genuinely equivalent non-biometric route.</li> <li><strong>Mobile Enrollment Units:</strong> Deployable teams may support enrollment of nomadic, unhoused, displaced, and remote populations, coordinated with Community Weavers and trusted local organizations. A mobile unit does not acquire civil-registration, citizenship, migration-status, or general identity authority by performing AUBI enrollment.</li></ul> <p><strong>AUBI Access Attestation Minimum:</strong> Each AUBI Access Attestation records or makes discoverable:</p> <ul><li>issuing AUBI body and constituting authority;</li> <li>AUBI-specific purpose and scope;</li> <li>evidence class and material uncertainty;</li> <li>permitted relying parties inside the AUBI payout and appeal chain;</li> <li>prohibited uses;</li> <li>validity, expiry, or review condition;</li> <li>claimant-controlled correction and challenge route;</li> <li>duplicate, collision, mistaken-merge, and mistaken-split handling;</li> <li>whether any cross-domain continuity recognition is prohibited, separately authorizable, or already authorized for a stated purpose.</li></ul> <p>The attestation is <strong>not</strong> a national ID, travel document, immigration credential, voting credential, general benefits key, employment identity, healthcare identity, justice identity, financial reputation score, or universal authentication token.</p> <p><strong>Cross-Domain Continuity:</strong></p> <ul><li>Another GGF framework may recognize that its local claimant record and an AUBI record concern the same person only under its own valid authority and CIS v0.2.4.</li> <li>Such recognition is purpose-limited and <strong>non-transitive by default</strong>. If AUBI is lawfully linked to Housing, and Housing is separately linked to Migration, AUBI does not thereby gain access to Migration records or authority to infer migration status.</li> <li>A continuity assertion links bounded records for a stated purpose; it does not merge source records into a master identity profile.</li> <li>Love Ledger participation, Gratitude Tokens, contribution history, LMCI-A evidence, LMCI-M data, Hearts activity, or reputation may not be used to strengthen or upgrade an AUBI Access Attestation into general identity.</li></ul> <p><strong>Payout Access:</strong></p> <ul><li><strong>Network of Access Points:</strong> Payouts are accessible not only through digital wallets but also via designated community centres, postal cash-out services, mobile money agents, trusted credit unions, and mobile service units.</li> <li><strong>No Fixed Address Required:</strong> Payout location does not affect eligibility.</li> <li><strong>Continuity Without Universal Identity:</strong> A person moving between participating AUBI regions may carry or re-establish the minimum evidence needed to continue an AUBI claim without requiring a universal correlation key. Any cross-region reconciliation follows the same correction, collision, minimum-disclosure, and challenge rules.</li></ul> <p><strong>External Status Changes:</strong></p> <ul><li>AUBI may receive a death record, legal-name correction, guardianship change, or other status transition established by a competent external authority where that event is relevant to AUBI administration.</li> <li>AUBI records the source, effective date, uncertainty or contest where applicable, and changes only consequences within its own lawful mandate.</li> <li>AUBI does not register the death, determine heirs, establish legal capacity, determine family status, or create another external legal consequence.</li> <li>Inactivity, wallet dormancy, non-response, disappearance from a residence, non-use of Hearts, cessation of Love Ledger participation, or missing telemetry does <strong>not</strong> establish death.</li></ul> <p><strong>Death and Unresolved AUBI Duties:</strong> An externally established death may end future baseline eligibility or account access as provided by applicable law, but it does not automatically:</p> <ul><li>erase accrued but unpaid AUBI amounts;</li> <li>erase pending correction, appeal, restoration, or wrongful-denial claims;</li> <li>erase payment-system or administrative liabilities;</li> <li>determine heirs, beneficiaries, or successors;</li> <li>delete records required for lawful audit, remedy, or succession;</li> <li>close unresolved duties merely because the claimant has died.</li></ul> <p>Any succession question is referred to the competent succession, probate, beneficiary, Indigenous, customary, contractual, judicial, or other authority. AUBI gains no succession jurisdiction by making the referral.</p> <p><strong>Safeguards:</strong></p> <ul><li><strong>Non-Discrimination Clause:</strong> Explicitly prohibits profiling or exclusion based on ethnicity, lifestyle, mobility, documentation, migration, housing, or disability status.</li> <li><strong>Purpose-Limited Data Governance:</strong> Enrollment and continuity data remain claimant-centred, minimally disclosed, and governed by Aurora v1.3.2 and applicable law, with strict prohibitions on secondary use by immigration enforcement, unrelated law enforcement, commercial credit, reputation scoring, or other surveillance systems absent separately valid authority.</li> <li><strong>Correction Before Consequence:</strong> Where identity continuity is materially disputed, avoidable adverse action pauses while mistaken merge, split, duplicate, collision, or factual error is reviewed. Essential access that does not require the disputed fact continues.</li> <li><strong>Missing-Population Review:</strong> BAZs must estimate and investigate eligible but unenrolled populations through the privacy-preserving sentinel in Appendix G.4; administrative enrolment totals alone cannot establish universal access and the sentinel must not become a compulsory registry of the unseen.</li></ul> <h4>5.1.2 Material Security Estimate and Hearts Usability & Freedom Test</h4> <p>A regional price basket is a starting observation, not a complete estimate of material security. Before setting or changing the baseline amount or fiat/Hearts ratio, the responsible body must assess:</p> <ul><li>actual availability, accessibility, quality, and waiting time for essential goods and services;</li> <li>household composition, dependency, debt and arrears, disability-related costs, and geographic burdens;</li> <li>the share of essential expenditure that remains fiat-only;</li> <li>Hearts acceptance, failed payment attempts, conversion demand, and geographic/provider coverage;</li> <li>whether unspent Hearts coexist with fiat hardship;</li> <li>subgroup effects, including impacts on marginalized, mobile, disabled, and digitally excluded participants;</li> <li>uncertainty, missing populations, and alternative explanations.</li></ul> <p>A higher Hearts share is inadmissible where it would reduce practical freedom to secure housing, food, utilities, transport, healthcare, or other essential needs. Ratio changes must be reversible, locally differentiated where justified, and reviewed against independent material-security outcomes. Changes in Hearts velocity alone do not establish that the composition is appropriate.</p> <h4>5.1.3 Baseline Reduction, Interruption, and Exit</h4> <p>Adding a baseline and removing one are not mirror operations. A household that has arranged housing, care, work, dependants, and debt around a baseline is exposed by its removal in a way that a household which never received it is not, and that exposure concentrates in exactly the households the baseline exists to protect. Reduction and interruption therefore carry procedural requirements that initial enrolment does not.</p> <p><strong>Policy-level reduction.</strong> No change lowering the Layer 1 amount, the fiat share, or the effective purchasing power of the baseline may take effect without:</p> <ul><li>a State Estimate Record under §4.5.2, including the material-security evidence relied on and the viable alternative explanations considered;</li> <li>a published reason and a stated reversal condition;</li> <li>a notice period no shorter than three disbursement intervals;</li> <li>phased rather than single-step implementation, where the reduction exceeds a threshold published in advance of the decision;</li> <li>accessible correction, human review, and appeal under §4.5.5.</li></ul> <p>Reductions are not retroactive. Recovery of an administrative overpayment may not reduce a person’s disbursement below the Material Security Estimate for their circumstances, and may not be recovered from Layer 3 needs-based support.</p> <p><strong>Individual interruption.</strong> Disbursement to an enrolled person may be suspended, withheld, or reduced only on grounds published in advance. It may not be suspended on the basis of:</p> <ul><li>non-response, wallet dormancy, missing telemetry, or absence from a residence;</li> <li>lapse or renewal failure of an AUBI Access Attestation, where the person remains identifiable through the correction route;</li> <li>suspicion or an open anomaly investigation — the §5.2 rule applies by analogy, in that an anomaly flag may open an investigation but may never itself reduce a benefit;</li> <li>non-participation in any contribution, care, restorative, treatment, employment, or training process;</li> <li>a criminal record, charge, or pending proceeding;</li> <li>refusal of Layer 2 or Layer 3 assessment.</li></ul> <p>Where an interruption is contested, the correction-before-consequence rule in §5.1.1 applies: essential access that does not depend on the disputed fact continues while the dispute is reviewed.</p> <p><strong>Custody and institutional residence.</strong> Where a person enters custody, detention, hospital, or institutional care and a competent authority is meeting the same material needs, the baseline may be adjusted for the period of that provision. In that case:</p> <ul><li>the adjustment records the expected end date and the reinstatement route;</li> <li>reinstatement takes effect on the day the person leaves, not on the day they apply;</li> <li>the reinstatement route is established before release wherever the holding authority will coordinate, and where it declines, non-reinstatement on the day of release is an unresolved AUBI duty that does not lapse;</li> <li>accrued Layer 3 entitlements, pending corrections, and pending appeals are unaffected.</li></ul> <p>AUBI acquires no custodial, supervisory, or compliance relationship by making this adjustment, and may not receive, hold, or verify a condition of release. Kintsugi §6.9 provides the care side of the same transition. Neither framework supervises the person.</p> <p><strong>Exit.</strong> Where eligibility genuinely ends — departure from a participating region, or a lawful determination that another jurisdiction’s floor applies — the baseline phases out across at least the notice period rather than stopping on a single date, and the person is told the reinstatement route before the first reduced disbursement.</p> <p><strong>Pilot and stage termination.</strong> A pilot, stage, bootstrapping arrangement, or Proto-Treasury that ends is a reduction under this section for every person receiving it. Termination and wind-down planning must form part of the pilot’s constituting decision under §7.1, not be deferred to its final months. A pilot that cannot state in advance what happens to participants when it ends is not ready to enrol them.</p> <h3>5.2 Layer 2: Contribution & Development Bonus</h3> <ul><li><strong>Purpose:</strong> Rewards verified contributions scored via <strong>LMCI-Allocation (LMCI-A)</strong>, paid in Hearts. LMCI-A operates independently from LMCI-M and the Love Ledger Narrative Feed. Contribution Bonus as <strong>Dignity Recognition</strong> explicitly recognizes work that market systems undervalue but human flourishing requires, while preserving the distinction between contribution recognition and human worth.</li> <li><strong>Contributions:</strong> <ul><li><strong>Love:</strong> Caregiving, mentorship, conflict resolution.</li> <li><strong>Meaning:</strong> Knowledge sharing, cultural preservation, spiritual care.</li> <li><strong>Connection:</strong> Ecological restoration, community organizing, and land stewardship; ecological outcome claims use Leaves and the Green Job Score under FLP and slow-variable safeguards.</li></ul></li> <li><strong>Dynamic Caps:</strong> Bonuses may be capped at 50% of baseline for the top income quintile, requiring greater demonstrated community impact and subject to transparent methodology and appeal.</li> <li>Layer 2 bonuses are earned by formal, <strong>chartered Community Providers</strong> (<code>institution_community_provider</code>). Citizens spend <code>Hearts</code> at these providers, which may redeem accumulated <code>Hearts</code> through the <strong>Hearts Treasury</strong> (<code>mechanism_hearts_treasury</code>) for necessary fiat costs.</li></ul> <h4>Proof of Care Evidence Layers</h4> <p>The <strong>Proof of Care</strong> (<code>process_proof_of_care</code>) record must specify which claim is being validated:</p> <ol><li><strong>Proof of Activity:</strong> the contribution occurred;</li> <li><strong>Recipient or Community Acceptance:</strong> the contribution was received as represented, where such confirmation is appropriate and safe;</li> <li><strong>Proof of Outcome:</strong> a relevant condition changed;</li> <li><strong>Proof of Additionality:</strong> the claimed outcome is attributable to the contribution rather than ordinary background change;</li> <li><strong>Uncertainty and Adverse Effects:</strong> limits, disagreement, unintended harm, or evidence that the contribution displaced other value.</li></ol> <p>Not every contribution requires all five layers. The system must not silently translate proof of activity into proof of outcome or additionality.</p> <h4>Gaming, Anomaly, and Appeal Safeguards</h4> <ul><li>AI anomaly detection may open an investigation but may never itself establish fraud, reduce a benefit, revoke a charter, or create a negative record.</li> <li>Community audits must disclose observer dependencies; the same person or body should not be the sole generator, validator, and adjudicator of a claim.</li> <li>Contribution decay (e.g., 5% annually) and scoring changes are transparent, versioned, and appealable.</li> <li>Providers and affected participants have the epistemic rights in §4.5.5, including access to evidence, contextual submission, human review, and correction.</li></ul> <h3>5.3 Layer 3: Equity & Needs-Based Support</h3> <ul><li><strong>Purpose:</strong> Provides needs-based support and delivers Treaty reparations for historical and ecological injustice. Layer 3 is <strong>Reparative and Substantive Equity</strong>, not a contribution reward and not an individual LMCI score.</li> <li><strong>Delivery:</strong> Support may be paid in Hearts, fiat, services, or a hybrid composition according to the actual need and the Hearts Usability & Freedom Test. A supplement must not be unusable for the condition it is intended to address.</li> <li><strong>Separation:</strong> Layer 3 evidence is held in a separate claimant-centred pipeline from LMCI-A, provider scoring, fraud analytics, and the Love Ledger Narrative Feed.</li></ul> <h4>5.3.1 Distinct Assessment Pathways</h4> <p><strong>Health and Disability Support:</strong> Uses self-report, functional-impact descriptions, trusted community attestation, professional evidence where available, assistive-cost information, and claimant-controlled review. A formal medical diagnosis is not the sole admissible path where inaccessible, culturally inappropriate, or unavailable.</p> <p><strong>Caregiving Burden Support:</strong> Assesses time and intensity of care, number and needs of dependants, health and sleep effects, opportunity cost, available respite, voluntariness, and household resources. Love Ledger recognition may not substitute for material support.</p> <p><strong>Geographic Equity:</strong> Assesses actual local costs, travel time, service availability, remoteness, infrastructure, climate exposure, supply volatility, and culturally specific access—not price differences alone.</p> <p><strong>Displacement, Migration, and Mobility:</strong> Uses claimant-controlled, purpose-limited AUBI access, entitlement, and continuity records. Portability means continuity of the AUBI claim across participating processes, not creation of a general identity credential. Any cross-domain same-person recognition follows CIS v0.2.4 and Aurora v1.3.2 and is non-transitive by default. LMCI-M is population-level and LMCI-A is contribution-level; neither may become a portable migrant or refugee score.</p> <p><strong>Collective Reparations:</strong> Requires recognition of the harmed community, evidence of historical and continuing harm, community-defined repair priorities, FPIC 2.0, collective governance, protection against individualizing collective harm, and long-term outcome review.</p> <h4>5.3.2 Assessment Principles</h4> <ul><li>Evidence is plural, proportionate, minimally intrusive, and interpreted with the claimant rather than solely about the claimant.</li> <li>Lack of conventional documentation must not be treated as lack of need.</li> <li>Decision-makers must record uncertainty and alternative interpretations.</li> <li>Where a condition is mixed or changing, support may be provisional and reviewed without presuming fraud.</li> <li>Claimants have accessible correction, explanation, human-review, and appeal rights.</li></ul> <h4>5.3.3 Negative Adjustments</h4> <p>Negative adjustments of up to 50% may be applied only for adjudicated Ecocide or serious human-rights violations by the <strong>Digital Justice Tribunal</strong>, with:</p> <ul><li>clear attribution and a high evidentiary threshold;</li> <li>advance notice and access to the evidence;</li> <li>representation and culturally appropriate process;</li> <li>proportionality, time limits, and a restoration pathway;</li> <li>independent appeal;</li> <li>protection of dependants and unrelated household members from collective punishment.</li></ul> <p>Algorithmic flags, political allegation, aggregate BAZ performance, or unresolved causal inference cannot authorize a negative adjustment.</p> <hr> <h2>6 Economic Architecture: Hearts and Leaves as Lifeblood</h2> <h3>6.1 Revenue Portfolio</h3> <ul><li><strong>Automation Tax:</strong> 5-25% on >30% productivity gains or >10% labor displacement, enforced by Treaty P4.</li> <li><strong>Resource Taxes:</strong> Carbon pricing, extraction fees, land value capture.</li> <li><strong>Commons Dividends:</strong> Digital/natural commons revenues.</li> <li><strong>Leaves Funding:</strong> Treaty P4 carbon/resource taxes for ecological restoration.</li></ul> <h3>6.2 Hearts and Leaves Currency</h3> <ul><li><strong>Hearts:</strong> Currency for care and community contributions, stabilized by P4 reserve fund, pegged to regenerative assets.</li> <li><strong>Leaves:</strong> Non-fungible tokens for ecological restoration, logged in Love Ledger.</li> <li><strong>Business Integration:</strong> Hearts/Leaves accepted for care economy markets, with tax incentives (up to 5%) for adoption.</li> <li><strong>Circulation Incentives:</strong> Hearts may use a bounded <strong>Demurrage Protocol</strong> (e.g., 0.5% monthly decay) to discourage strategic hoarding, but demurrage may not be introduced or increased solely because aggregate velocity is low or balances are dormant. The Low Hearts Velocity Diagnostic (§7.4) must first exclude access, acceptance, trust, concentration, seasonality, informal substitution, sufficiency, and recording failure. Leaves remain exempt from demurrage as long-horizon ecological records.</li></ul> <h3>6.3 Stress Testing</h3> <ul><li><strong>Inflation (>10%):</strong> Shift to baseline-only, deploy P4 reserves.</li> <li><strong>Recession:</strong> Increase AUBI payouts via counter-cyclical stabilizers.</li> <li><strong>Inequality:</strong> Gradual rollout to prevent migration pressures.</li> <li><strong>Cryptocurrency Volatility:</strong> Hearts stability via P4 reserve fund, temporary trading halts.</li> <li><strong>Observation Failure:</strong> If transaction records, provider reports, participant reports, or ecological channels conflict or show compromised provenance, suspend state-specific intervention and invoke independent verification under §4.5.</li> <li><strong>Indicator Self-Validation:</strong> Any intervention that directly changes its trigger metric must be evaluated against independent material, relational, distributive, and ecological outcomes.</li></ul> <h3>6.4 The Hearts Treasury: A Stability Anchor</h3> <p>Its function as a financial clearinghouse, managed in partnership with local credit unions, that guarantees fiat redemption for <code>Hearts</code> at a stable rate. It is initially capitalized by the <strong>Global Commons Fund (GCF)</strong> (<code>institution_gcf</code>) and sustained by levies, as detailed in the white paper.</p> <h3>6.5 Countering Elite Exit and Parallel Systems</h3> <p>A recognized risk is that wealthy actors and corporations may ignore the <em>Hearts</em> economy, operating purely in fiat and forming a parallel dominant system that undermines the regenerative transition. The AUBI framework does not attempt to solve this through coercion; instead, it relies on <strong>pull mechanisms</strong> embedded in the broader GGF ecosystem:</p> <table><thead><tr><th><strong>Countermeasure</strong></th><th><strong>GGF Mechanism</strong></th><th><strong>Effect</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Global Taxation</strong></td><td>Treaty for Our Only Home, Pillar 4</td><td>Carbon taxes, financial transaction taxes, and digital services levies apply to <em>all</em> economic activity regardless of <em>Hearts</em> participation. Elites cannot exit the extractive taxation regime.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Regenerative Trade Zone (RTZ)</strong></td><td>Gaian Trade Framework</td><td>Preferential market access and reduced tariffs for entities meeting regenerative standards (including <em>Hearts</em>/<em>Leaves</em> integration). Non-participants face trade friction.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Corporate Certification Pressure</strong></td><td>Regenerative Enterprise Framework</td><td><strong>Leadership Pay Equity</strong> (10:1 ratio), <strong>Reparations Index</strong>, and <strong>Digital Product Passports</strong> create reputational and investor pressure. Public certification is visible on the <strong>Public Trust Dashboard</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Anchor Procurement</strong></td><td>Treaty Pillar 2 / Municipal Finance Bridge</td><td>Participating Anchor Institutions (municipalities, hospitals, universities) commit to sourcing a percentage of procurement from <em>Hearts</em>-based supply chains, creating guaranteed demand.</td></tr></tbody></table> <p>These mechanisms are not enforced through AUBI alone; they are structural features of the Treaty and Gaian Trade architecture. The AUBI framework assumes their operation and integrates with them via the <strong>Social Resilience Council</strong> and <strong>Inter-Currency Translation Layer</strong>.</p> <hr> <h2>7 Implementation Sequencing</h2> <h3>7.1 Pilot Site Selection</h3> <ul><li><strong>Criteria (100-point scale):</strong> Economic diversity (25), political stability (25), cultural representation (30, prioritizing Indigenous presence), technical readiness (20).</li> <li><strong>Priority Regions:</strong> Indigenous Canada (80/100), Urban Sweden (92/100), Rural Kenya (78/100), Bangladesh (76/100), Urban India (72/100).</li> <li><strong>Liberation-First Piloting:</strong> Initial implementations prioritized in communities most severely impacted by the three-pillar system:</li> <li>Indigenous communities facing resource extraction and cultural shame</li> <li>Urban poor trapped in debt and survival anxiety</li> <li>Regions with strong hierarchical control systems</li> <li>Youth populations burdened by education debt and performance shame</li></ul> <p>A pilot’s constituting decision must state, before enrolment, what happens to participants if the pilot ends, is not renewed, or is rolled back: the notice period, the phasing schedule, the reinstatement route into any successor arrangement, and which body holds the unresolved duty. Termination planning is an enrolment prerequisite under §5.1.3, not a closing task.</p> <h3>7.2 Technology Rollout</h3> <ul><li><strong>Year 1:</strong> Hyperledger Fabric deployment, Love Ledger beta, mobile app launch.</li> <li><strong>Year 2:</strong> Hearts/Leaves launch, Treaty integration, LMCI validation.</li> <li><strong>Year 3:</strong> DeFi integration, Indigenous-led AI governance.</li></ul> <h3>7.3 Staff Training</h3> <ul><li><strong>Community Weavers (6 months):</strong> Train in LMCI distinctions, Right Relationship, Indigenous protocols, community engagement, low-tech implementation, uncertainty, causal alternatives, accessibility, and the epistemic rights in §4.5. Community Weavers (<code>process_community_weaver</code>) support participants and Community Providers but are not presumed neutral or omniscient observers.</li> <li><strong>Role Separation:</strong> A Community Weaver may originate or attest evidence but may not be the sole validator and adjudicator of the same consequential claim. Secondary review is required for adverse, high-value, disputed, or structurally consequential decisions.</li> <li><strong>Capacity Monitoring:</strong> BAZs publish privacy-preserving Weaver caseload, validation backlog, turnover, translation capacity, complaint response, and community evaluation metrics under Appendix G.6.</li> <li><strong>Scaling:</strong> 500 Community Weavers (Year 1), 2,000 (Year 2), 10,000+ (Year 3), subject to demonstrated training and supervision capacity rather than numerical targets alone.</li></ul> <h3>7.4 The Grassroots Bootstrapping Pathway (The BAZ Starter Pack)</h3> <p>Hearts faces a classic cold-start problem: the currency has no useful circulation until people can spend it, while providers have little reason to accept it before demand and redemption are credible. This section moves from social trust to monetary function in four stages. Each stage has explicit entry and exit criteria; no stage should be skipped.</p> <h4>Stage 1: The Convening Circle</h4> <p>A small group (minimum 5, recommended 8–15 people) identifies a concrete, recurring service need in their community—something people currently pay for, meet informally, or go without. They commit to meeting that need using a simple shared ledger before advanced technology is introduced.</p> <p><em>Minimum observation stack:</em> completed and failed exchanges, disputes, unmet requests, reason an exchange failed, and participant/non-participant reflections. These records describe the circle; they are not representative LMCI-M evidence.</p> <p><em>Entry criterion:</em> 5+ participants, one identified service need, one designated record-keeper.</p> <p><em>Exit criterion:</em> At least 20 completed service exchanges over a minimum 60-day period, with disputes documented and resolved or explicitly carried forward. Zero disputes is not required; silent or inaccessible dispute channels must not be mistaken for trust.</p> <p>The 60-day minimum prevents enthusiasm spikes from being mistaken for stable use. Service exchanges must represent real time, labour, goods, materials, or support rather than circular or symbolic transactions created to satisfy the threshold.</p> <h4>Stage 2: The Proto-Treasury</h4> <p>The circle opens a joint bank account or equivalent community fund seeded with fiat, grant money, or in-kind contributions. This becomes the backing reserve for the first Hearts issued. Fiat reserves must cover at least 30% of outstanding Hearts at face value unless a formally declared and time-limited crisis rule under Financial Systems applies.</p> <p>The Heartstarter program (Financial Systems §5) provides a 1:1 fiat match on Proto-Treasury seed capital up to a per-BAZ ceiling, drawing from the Global Commons Fund.</p> <p><em>Minimum observation stack additions:</em> issuance and reserve records, eligible use transactions, failed transactions, offline reconciliation, provider capacity, redemption reasons, access barriers, and wallet-balance distribution in privacy-preserving form.</p> <p><em>Entry criterion:</em> Stage 1 exit criteria met; at least one Community Provider recruited and willing to accept Hearts.</p> <p><em>Exit criterion:</em> Proto-Treasury reserves ≥30% of all Hearts issued; at least 3 distinct Community Providers accepting Hearts; minimum 100 eligible use transactions across at least 20 distinct participants; no unresolved material data-integrity failure.</p> <h4>Stage 3: Provider Network Expansion</h4> <p>The circle recruits additional Community Providers, targeting practical diversity and redundancy across care, food, repair, education, ecological, and locally identified needs. A nominal provider category does not establish adequate capacity, accessibility, quality, or geographic coverage.</p> <h5>Velocity Definition and Decomposition</h5> <p>For Stage 3, <strong>use velocity</strong> is eligible Hearts transaction value during the observation window divided by average eligible Hearts stock during that window. Eligible use includes genuine purchases and service exchange. It excludes initial issuance, Treasury redemption, ICTL conversion without an associated good or service, self-transfers, transfers among commonly controlled wallets, reversals, administrative migrations, and known wash or fraudulent transactions.</p> <p>The dashboard must separately report:</p> <ul><li>use velocity;</li> <li>recirculation after first use;</li> <li>Treasury redemption rate;</li> <li>active-participant share;</li> <li>90-day dormancy share;</li> <li>balance and dormancy concentration;</li> <li>data-integrity and offline-reconciliation status.</li></ul> <h5>Low Hearts Velocity Diagnostic</h5> <blockquote><p><strong>Low Hearts velocity is an alert signal, not a diagnosis.</strong></p></blockquote> <p>A use velocity below one eligible exchange per 30 days over a rolling 90-day window triggers diagnostic review but does not by itself authorize changes to issuance, demurrage, the fiat/Hearts ratio, provider recruitment, or participant incentives.</p> <p>Before intervention, the responsible BAZ body must assess:</p> <ol><li>transaction-data integrity and offline reconciliation;</li> <li>seasonal, climatic, migratory, and cultural rhythms;</li> <li>provider-category diversity, practical capacity, waiting time, and redundancy;</li> <li>geographic, temporal, linguistic, disability, and digital access;</li> <li>unmet service demand and failed requests;</li> <li>essential-goods Hearts acceptance and fiat-essential burden;</li> <li>wallet-balance and dormant-balance concentration;</li> <li>participant trust, provider quality, and rule stability;</li> <li>informal or non-monetized substitution and informal-carer burden;</li> <li>material sufficiency and whether lower formal exchange is locally desired.</li></ol> <p>The review must produce a State Estimate Record under §4.5. Where several causes coexist, the record must identify affected subgroups and intervention order. Where evidence remains compatible with conflicting responses, the finding is <strong>Cause unresolved</strong>.</p> <p>A policy may not certify itself by moving velocity. Demurrage, forced spending, additional issuance, or provider transactions may increase the indicator while reducing trust, material security, or relational wellbeing. Independent outcomes must therefore be monitored.</p> <p><em>Entry criterion:</em> Stage 2 exit criteria met.</p> <p><em>Exit criterion:</em> Minimum 5 practically functioning provider categories; average use velocity ≥1 eligible exchange per 30 days over a 90-day window; 30-day fraud rate <code>&lt;2%</code> by transaction value; a trained Community Weaver with secondary-review access; operational low-velocity diagnostic channels; and no unresolved access, data-integrity, or administrative-capacity failure that would make the threshold misleading.</p> <p>A community with lower velocity caused by documented sufficiency, seasonality, or voluntary non-monetized provision is not pressured to increase transactions. It may remain at Stage 3, adapt the monetary scale, or choose not to proceed to formalization.</p> <h4>Stage 4: Governance Formalization</h4> <p>Only after Stage 3 exit criteria are met does a BAZ Council form. Governance imposed before a functioning economy exists tends to govern phantom activity and attract participants motivated by governance power rather than community value creation.</p> <p>The BAZ Council election uses §4.1. Upon formalization, the BAZ applies to the Social Resilience Council for transition to Phase 2 cryptographic infrastructure under Financial Systems §4.3.</p> <p><em>Formalization criteria (all must be satisfied):</em></p> <ul><li>≥500 distinct AUBI-enrolled participants active in the Hearts economy;</li> <li>reserve ratio maintained ≥30% for 6 consecutive months;</li> <li>fraud rate <code>&lt;1%</code> by transaction value over the preceding 90 days;</li> <li>at least one successful LMCI-A reporting cycle with functioning correction and appeal;</li> <li>explicit outreach to marginalized populations, plus a Missing-Population Sentinel estimate rather than enrolment counts alone;</li> <li>published Observation Contracts for consequential local decisions;</li> <li>functioning State Estimate Record, individual epistemic-rights, and administrative-capacity processes;</li> <li>an Observation Independence Map identifying shared observers, platforms, and incentives.</li></ul> <p><em>Phase 2 graduation criteria:</em></p> <ul><li>≥2,000 active participants;</li> <li>continuous operation for ≥12 months post-formalization;</li> <li>reserve ratio maintained ≥30% throughout, absent a formally documented crisis exception;</li> <li>Phase 1 transaction data validated as forward-compatible with Phase 2 schemas;</li> <li>no unresolved high-severity observation, appeal, exclusion, or governance-capacity failure.</li></ul> <h4>Fallback Protocol</h4> <p>If a BAZ fails to meet Stage 3 exit criteria within 18 months of Stage 2 entry, the process does not automatically fail. It triggers an independent structured review of provider diversity, capacity, access, quality, currency acceptance, trust, distribution, seasonality, informal substitution, technical integrity, fraud, reserves, and administrative capacity. Community Weavers contribute evidence but may not perform the review alone.</p> <p>Heartstarter match funds are not clawed back on failure. They remain in the Proto-Treasury to support a revised attempt, a lower-scale design, or orderly wind-down. Failure and unresolved causation are documented in the Failure Library without forcing a diagnosis unsupported by evidence.</p> <h3>7.5 Transition Shock Absorption</h3> <p>External critique correctly identifies that rapid AUBI rollout could disrupt labor markets, collapse low-status jobs, and trigger political backlash—even if long-term outcomes are positive. The GGF ecosystem includes dedicated transition architecture to absorb this shock:</p> <table><thead><tr><th><strong>Transition Risk</strong></th><th><strong>Mitigation Mechanism</strong></th><th><strong>GGF Framework</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Labor market displacement in extractive sectors</td><td><strong>Industrial Sunsetting</strong> with time-bound phase-out schedules and <strong>Just Transition Fund</strong> retraining support</td><td>Work in Liberation Framework</td></tr><tr><td>Gig worker precarity during transition</td><td><strong>Gig Worker Transition Pathway</strong> — dual participation allowed during phase-in, with <em>Hearts</em> bonuses for shift to Community Work Teams</td><td>Work in Liberation Framework</td></tr><tr><td>Wage spikes in essential but undesirable sectors</td><td><strong>Temporary Wage Subsidies</strong> and <strong>Critical Work Guarantees</strong> (healthcare, logistics, food systems) coordinated by Social Resilience Council</td><td>Financial Systems Framework</td></tr><tr><td>Regional fiscal stress in transition-heavy areas</td><td><strong>Lawfully constituted transitional finance</strong> — time-limited debt-service pauses, liquidity facilities, or restructuring under Treaty Article 19.4 and valid financial protocols. Debt-for-regeneration conversion is deferred under Financial Systems §15.8 and Treaty §19.3 and is not available as a transition buffer</td><td>Treaty for Our Only Home, Financial Systems Framework</td></tr><tr><td>Political backlash from displaced industries</td><td><strong>Just Transition Compacts</strong> — legal agreements requiring companies to support worker retraining, enforceable by Digital Justice Tribunal</td><td>Treaty for Our Only Home, Pillar 3</td></tr><tr><td>Housing-cost capitalization of the transfer</td><td><strong>Capitalization observation</strong> — whether purchasing power gains are reaching residents or the owners of the land beneath them, observed as a housing-market outcome; remedy selection remains with the competent land, tax, or rent authority</td><td>Housing & Secure Habitation §10.1</td></tr><tr><td>Withdrawal shock at pilot termination, stage rollback, or individual exit</td><td><strong>Reduction, interruption, and exit procedure</strong> — notice, phasing, non-retroactivity, an overpayment-recovery floor, and reinstatement-on-release; termination planning required in the pilot’s constituting decision</td><td>AUBI §5.1.3, Kintsugi §6.9</td></tr></tbody></table> <p>The AUBI rollout is sequenced to activate these buffers <em>before</em> full-scale implementation. Pilots (Section 7.1) are deliberately placed in communities already facing transition pressure, allowing buffer mechanisms to be tested and refined.</p> <h3>7.6 If You Only Implement 30%: Minimum Viable AUBI</h3> <p>The full AUBI framework is architecturally complex by design—it anticipates capture, drift, and gaming across multiple scales. However, this complexity creates a recognized risk: <strong>partial implementation may distort the system and trigger political backlash before it stabilizes.</strong> This section provides explicit guidance for communities, municipalities, or BAZs that can only implement a fraction of the framework.</p> <p><strong>The Golden Rule of Partial Implementation:</strong></p> <blockquote><p><em>Start with the Convening Circle (Stage 1). Do not attempt to implement the Hearts Treasury, LMCI dashboards, or cryptographic infrastructure until Stage 3 exit criteria are met. The system is designed to scale with demonstrated value, not to be deployed fully formed.</em></p></blockquote> <p><strong>Minimum Viable Stack (30% Implementation):</strong></p> <table><thead><tr><th><strong>If you can only do this…</strong></th><th><strong>Prioritize this…</strong></th><th><strong>And explicitly defer this…</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>One community group (5–15 people)</strong></td><td>Convening Circle (Stage 1). Identify one recurring need. Use a simple shared ledger (paper or spreadsheet). Build trust through repeated exchange.</td><td>Hearts Treasury, cryptographic verification, LMCI dashboards, formal BAZ governance.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>One neighborhood or small town (50–500 people)</strong></td><td>Convening Circle → Proto-Treasury (Stage 2). Open a joint account. Issue simple local credits backed by the Proto-Treasury reserve (minimum 30%). Recruit 3+ Community Providers.</td><td>Inter-Currency Translation Layer, Global Commons Fund integration, Social Resilience Council oversight.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>One municipality or BAZ candidate (500–2,000 people)</strong></td><td>Proto-Treasury → Provider Network Expansion (Stage 3). Achieve practical provider diversity, maintain the reserve floor, operate the low-velocity diagnostic, monitor access and administrative capacity, and train at least one Community Weaver with secondary-review support.</td><td>Full LMCI-M, cryptographic decentralization (Phase 2), Treaty-level integration.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Full BAZ formalization (2,000+ people)</strong></td><td>Governance Formalization (Stage 4). Form BAZ Council, implement Observation Contracts, State Estimate Records, appeal rights, LMCI-M independence, and missing-population/admin-capacity sentinels. Transition to Phase 2 only after 12+ months of stable operation.</td><td>Global coordination, sovereign debt transformation, geopolitical strategy.</td></tr></tbody></table> <p><strong>What Partial Implementers Should <em>Not</em> Do:</strong></p> <ul><li>❌ <strong>Do not</strong> create a <em>Hearts</em>-like currency without a Proto-Treasury reserve. Unbacked local currencies collapse and damage trust in the broader model.</li> <li>❌ <strong>Do not</strong> attempt to measure LMCI-A contributions without first establishing Community Weaver validation. Premature metricization invites gaming and bureaucratization.</li> <li>❌ <strong>Do not</strong> form a BAZ Council before a functioning local economy exists (Stage 3 exit criteria). Governance without an economy attracts power-seeking behavior rather than value creation.</li> <li>❌ <strong>Do not</strong> change demurrage, issuance, or the fiat/Hearts ratio from aggregate velocity or dormancy alone. Diagnose the underlying state first.</li> <li>❌ <strong>Do not</strong> treat an increase in transactions, contribution claims, or dashboard scores as proof that material or relational conditions improved.</li></ul> <p><strong>Why This Works:</strong> The bootstrapping pathway (Section 7.4) is not a “nice to have”—it is the <strong>operating system kernel</strong>. The cryptographic, financial, and governance layers are <strong>peripheral extensions</strong> that become necessary only when the network exceeds the scale where social trust alone can coordinate. Communities operating below Dunbar’s number (approximately 150 people) may never need Phase 2 infrastructure.</p> <p><strong>Failure Is Expected and Accounted For:</strong></p> <ul><li>If a Convening Circle dissolves after 60 days without meeting exit criteria: <strong>document and share.</strong> This is valuable data for the Failure Library (Genesis Protocol §4.6).</li> <li>If a Proto-Treasury fails to maintain the 30% reserve ratio: <strong>issuance suspends automatically.</strong> The system degrades gracefully to Stage 1 rather than collapsing.</li> <li>If a BAZ fails to meet formalization criteria within 18 months: <strong>Heartstarter match funds are not clawed back.</strong> They remain for a revised attempt or orderly wind-down.</li></ul> <p>The AUBI framework is designed for <strong>graceful degradation and iterative learning</strong>, not brittle all-or-nothing deployment. Partial implementation, when sequenced correctly, is not a failure mode—it is the intended scaling pathway.</p> <hr> <h3>8 LMCI: Architecture and Measurement</h3> <p>This section specifies <strong>LMCI-Measurement (LMCI-M)</strong> as an independent population-level governance estimate. For LMCI-Allocation (LMCI-A), see §5.2. For operational telemetry and the Love Ledger Narrative Feed, see §2.3. Shared vocabulary does not permit shared data pipelines or silent aggregation.</p> <h3>8.1 LMCI-M Data Sources</h3> <ul><li><strong>Love:</strong> Stratified random population surveys at minimum annual cadence, sampled relationship-quality assessments, social-cohesion measures, and independent community research. Administration is institutionally separate from AUBI benefit and provider validation.</li> <li><strong>Meaning:</strong> Cultural-vitality reports from Indigenous and community councils, longitudinal knowledge-transmission tracking, voluntary purpose and engagement research, and accounts of cultural loss or renewal. These are not derived from individual provider logs.</li> <li><strong>Connection:</strong> Ecological monitoring, sacred-site health assessments, biodiversity indicators, and traditional observer reports co-interpreted with long-embedded communities under §4.3.</li> <li><strong>External Context:</strong> Material security, public health, distributional, ecological, and institutional indicators may contextualize LMCI-M without being silently absorbed into the index.</li></ul> <h3>8.2 Multi-Timescale Architecture</h3> <p>LMCI-M is not a single real-time number. Four products serve different decisions:</p> <ol><li><strong>Operational Pulse:</strong> High-frequency alerts concerning service interruption, distress, isolation, access failure, ecological events, or administrative overload. Operational pulses are not LMCI-M and cannot by themselves authorize long-term policy changes.</li> <li><strong>Quarterly Nowcast:</strong> A provisional, model-based estimate using partial current evidence. It is explicitly marked uncertain and revisable and must show which high-frequency proxies were used.</li> <li><strong>Annual LMCI-M Estimate:</strong> The primary population-level estimate based on independent sampling, cultural interpretation, ecological evidence, subgroup distribution, and published methodology.</li> <li><strong>Slow-Variable Register:</strong> Long-horizon cultural and ecological observations that may not yet support a scalar trend but require preservation in governance deliberation and, where applicable, LEKH standing.</li></ol> <p>Every product displays its as-of date, data age, population coverage, missingness, confidence or uncertainty range, subgroup divergence, methodological changes, and material dissent. Divergent timescales must remain visible rather than being compressed into one reassuring aggregate.</p> <h3>8.3 Validation, Uncertainty, and Divergence</h3> <ul><li><strong>Cultural Calibration:</strong> Indigenous-led and locally legitimate metric development with the right to maintain non-comparable local components where forced standardization would erase meaning.</li> <li><strong>Longitudinal Validation:</strong> Twenty-year tracking, comparison with GNH and OECD Better Life Index, and testing of whether LMCI-M predicts outcomes the framework claims to value.</li> <li><strong>Source Independence:</strong> Maintain and publish the Observation Independence Map for survey, ecological, community, and administrative channels.</li> <li><strong>Gaming Prevention:</strong> AI may flag anomalies; community whistleblowing, independent re-sampling, and multi-source verification determine whether distortion occurred. Automated anomaly detection does not establish misconduct.</li> <li><strong>Uncertainty:</strong> Publish intervals or qualitative confidence, data gaps, partial identification, competing interpretations, and known model limitations.</li> <li><strong>Method Changes:</strong> Survey, sampling, weighting, aggregation, and ecological-component changes are versioned and governed under §4.5.4. A score change caused by a method change must be separated from a change in the observed population.</li> <li><strong>Subgroup Protection:</strong> Aggregate improvement does not justify policy if materially harmed groups are concealed by the average.</li> <li><strong>Causal Restraint:</strong> LMCI-M movement does not by itself establish that AUBI caused the change. External shocks, reporting changes, lagged effects, and other policies remain viable explanations until assessed.</li></ul> <p><strong>LMCI-A / LMCI-M Divergence Protocol:</strong></p> <ul><li>Rising LMCI-A activity with stagnant or falling LMCI-M triggers inquiry into activity–outcome mismatch, benefit distribution, administrative burden, gaming, external harms, and measurement failure.</li> <li>Improving LMCI-M with low formal activity may indicate informal provision, delayed effects, or that formal transaction growth is unnecessary.</li> <li>No automatic policy response follows from divergence; a State Estimate Record is required.</li></ul> <h3>8.4 Success Metrics and Legitimacy</h3> <p><strong>Liberation Indicators:</strong></p> <ul><li>reduction in survival anxiety;</li> <li>increased practical ability to refuse exploitative conditions;</li> <li>career changes toward meaningful or freely chosen work;</li> <li>growth in voluntary community self-organization;</li> <li>decline in mental-health presentations related to performance shame;</li> <li>increased time for creative, relational, civic, and restorative activity;</li> <li>improved access to essential goods and services across marginalized subgroups;</li> <li>reduced unpaid-care exhaustion and administrative burden.</li></ul> <p><strong>Addressing the Post-Survival Meaning Gap:</strong> Removing survival pressure does not guarantee that every person will experience purpose. Some may experience existential drift, especially where identity was tied to achievement or status. AUBI therefore connects participants voluntarily to:</p> <ul><li><strong>Skills Resonance Matching</strong> and <strong>Community Work Teams</strong> under Work in Liberation;</li> <li>LMCI-M Meaning inquiry, which may trigger diagnostic review where cultural vitality or purpose conditions decline but does not prescribe a uniform adjustment;</li> <li>the <strong>Consciousness Alignment Index</strong> for voluntary developmental pathways;</li> <li><strong>Global Meaning Infrastructure</strong> as mental-health, civic, educational, artistic, and ethical public infrastructure.</li></ul> <p>AUBI guarantees conditions for purpose-seeking, not a centrally defined purpose.</p> <p><strong>Crisis Legitimacy and Emotional Trust:</strong> Metrics are necessary but insufficient during shocks, scandals, and failures. Complementary mechanisms include:</p> <ul><li><strong>Deliberative Narrative Protocol:</strong> A random sample of citizens produces a plain-language Citizen’s Rationale displayed prominently alongside uncertainty, dissent, and technical evidence.</li> <li><strong>Failure Library:</strong> Mandatory documentation of failed and unresolved pilots, including cases where the system could not determine causation.</li> <li><strong>Restorative Justice Circles:</strong> Community-led repair where implementation causes unintended harm.</li></ul> <p>These mechanisms provide scaffolding rather than a guarantee. Legitimacy is earned through transparent uncertainty, correctable decisions, visible accountability, and restraint when evidence is insufficient.</p> <hr> <h2>9 Cultural Integration</h2> <h3>9.1 Indigenous Sovereignty</h3> <ul><li><strong>FPIC 2.0 & Red Lines Clause:</strong> Communities can modify/withdraw participation.</li> <li><strong>Traditional Knowledge:</strong> Elders advise on AUBI design, with ceremonial governance.</li> <li><strong>Data Sovereignty:</strong> Indigenous communities control access, interpretation, transmission, retention, and withdrawal of their LMCI and traditional-knowledge data. Global comparability does not override community authority or sacredness boundaries.</li></ul> <h3>9.2 Religious & Cultural Sensitivity</h3> <ul><li><strong>Faith-Based Models:</strong> Sharia-compliant Hearts, Buddhist mindfulness, Indigenous spirituality.</li> <li><strong>Secular Inclusion:</strong> Humanist and atheist contributions recognized.</li></ul> <h3>9.3 Geopolitical Resilience and Defensive Strategy</h3> <p>Hostile state and non-state actors may attempt to weaponize AUBI implementation—framing it as destabilization, exploiting migration flows, or running disinformation campaigns. The AUBI framework itself does not contain geopolitical strategy; this resides in the broader GGF architecture:</p> <table><thead><tr><th><strong>Threat Vector</strong></th><th><strong>Defensive Mechanism</strong></th><th><strong>GGF Framework</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Disinformation campaigns (“collapse experiment,” “globalist takeover”)</td><td><strong>Synoptic Protocol</strong> and <strong>Public Epistemic Institutions</strong> — trusted, transparent information channels; <strong>Counter-Misinformation Teams</strong> with fact-checking partnerships</td><td>Treaty Pillar 5, Meta-Governance Framework</td></tr><tr><td>Migration weaponization (framing AUBI as “pull factor”)</td><td><strong>Sovereignty Bargains</strong> — nations retain control over enrollment criteria; <strong>Tiered Activation Pathways</strong> allow graduated participation</td><td>Treaty Implementation Strategies (Dual-Track Approach)</td></tr><tr><td>Economic sanctions or trade retaliation</td><td><strong>Regenerative Trade Zone (RTZ)</strong> — parallel trade infrastructure reduces dependency on hostile actors; <strong>Global Commons Fund</strong> provides fiscal buffers</td><td>Gaian Trade Framework, Treaty Pillar 4</td></tr><tr><td>Active sabotage (cyber, legal, narrative)</td><td><strong>Shield Protocol</strong> — investigates and counters transnational efforts to undermine GGF; <strong>Backlash Early-Warning System</strong> (AI monitoring of nationalist rhetoric); <strong>Office of the Adversary</strong> — permanent independent red team agency mandated to challenge proposals, identify risks, and publish dissenting reports alongside major policies; <strong>Planetary Immune System (PIS)</strong> — detects and responds to systemic threats including coordinated attacks on GGF infrastructure</td><td>Genesis Protocol, Treaty Pillar 5, Existential Risk Governance Framework</td></tr><tr><td>State-level non-cooperation</td><td><strong>Pragmatic & Geopolitical Track (The “Is”)</strong> — non-public playbook addressing P5 and G20 interests, sovereignty safeguards, and “golden parachutes” for phased-out roles</td><td>Treaty for Our Only Home, Implementation Strategies</td></tr></tbody></table> <p>The AUBI rollout assumes coordination with these defensive layers. Pilot regions are selected in part for their geopolitical insulation (e.g., sub-national BAZs in stable democracies) to minimize exposure during early phases.</p> <hr> <h2>10 Technical Architecture Overview</h2> <ul><li><strong>Blockchain:</strong> Hyperledger Fabric or functionally equivalent infrastructure, with Indigenous-controlled nodes in participating BAZs and staged deployment under the Bridge Protocol.</li> <li><strong>Security:</strong> Post-quantum migration planning, zero-trust architecture, role separation, and independent recovery paths.</li> <li><strong>Interoperability:</strong> Versioned APIs and schemas with GDPR-compatible data minimization and local data-sovereignty controls.</li> <li><strong>Decision Provenance:</strong> Every consequential record includes framework version, protocol version, data as-of date, observer provenance, estimator version, and later corrections.</li> <li><strong>Observation Separation:</strong> LMCI-M, LMCI-A, operational telemetry, Layer 3 evidence, Love Ledger Narrative Feed, and AUBI access/continuity records are logically and access-controlled as separate systems. Cross-system access is explicit, purpose-limited, and auditable.</li> <li><strong>No Universal Correlation Key:</strong> AUBI infrastructure must not require one persistent identifier across finance, Love Ledger, migration, housing, health, justice, work, or civic systems. Pairwise continuity recognition is separately authorized and non-transitive by default.</li> <li><strong>Source-Preserving Correction:</strong> Mistaken merge, mistaken split, duplicate, collision, and identifier correction are represented without silently rewriting another framework’s source record.</li> <li><strong>Human Review:</strong> Automated systems may summarize, flag, or model evidence but may not issue final adverse findings without accountable human review and appeal.</li> <li><strong>Graceful Degradation:</strong> Where technical systems fail, offline rights and service access continue; policy decisions based on corrupted or unreconciled records are suspended rather than guessed.</li></ul> <hr> <h2>11 Communication Strategy</h2> <ul><li><strong>Narrative:</strong> AUBI as Right Relationship, enabling flourishing through Indigenous wisdom.</li> <li><strong>Campaigns:</strong> #AUBIForHumanity, Indigenous-led storytelling, interactive documentaries.</li> <li><strong>Criticism Response:</strong> Address work disincentives, cultural imperialism with evidence from pilots and FPIC protections.</li> <li><strong>AUBI:</strong> From Extraction to Coordination — Economic Systems That Serve People</li> <li>Hearts: A Currency Designed for Care, Not Capture</li> <li>Love Ledger: Recognizing What Markets Can’t See</li> <li>From Compliance to Contribution: What Happens When Survival Is Secure</li></ul> <hr> <h2>12 Conclusion</h2> <p>AUBI is the beating heart of a regenerative civilization, channeling Hearts and Leaves to nurture Right Relationship, guided by Indigenous wisdom and protected by Treaty-aligned governance. It transforms material liberation into a platform for planetary flourishing while recognizing that adaptive governance must remain explicit about what it can observe, what it cannot yet distinguish, and when uncertainty requires restraint. It is ready for bounded, observability-hardened pilot development in Indigenous-led BAZs.</p> <p><strong>Next Steps:</strong></p> <ul><li>Develop pilot systems in BAZs.</li> <li>Engage Indigenous leaders, Treaty partners, and City Networks.</li> <li>Launch #AUBIForHumanity campaign emphasizing Right Relationship.</li></ul> <h3>12.1 Acknowledged Irreducible Tensions</h3> <p>The AUBI framework, and the broader GGF ecosystem within which it operates, is not a claim to have “solved” the deepest tensions of governing complex human systems. Several tensions are <strong>irreducible by design</strong>—they cannot be eliminated, only named, structurally bounded, and monitored. This section makes those tensions explicit.</p> <h4>1. Cultural Renewal vs. Procedural Governance</h4> <p><strong>The Tension:</strong> Audits, governance loops, and drift detection mechanisms are <em>rational</em> tools. But culture does not drift because of a lack of audits; it drifts because <strong>meaning erodes</strong>. Over decades, the symbolic, emotional, and identity-forming dimensions of the system may hollow out, leaving technocratically correct but spiritually empty institutions.</p> <p><strong>Containment Strategy:</strong> The GGF ecosystem includes cultural infrastructure—<strong>Global Meaning Infrastructure</strong> (Treaty Pillar 5), <strong>Cultural Heritage Framework</strong>, <strong>Arts Flourishing Framework</strong>, and the <strong>Meta-Governance philosophical treatise</strong> on “Governance from Being.” However, these elements are inherently less operationalizable than economic mechanisms. The framework acknowledges this asymmetry and commits to periodic <strong>citizen-led cultural renewal assemblies</strong> (not technocratic reviews) as a counterweight. Effectiveness depends on ongoing community ownership, not technical enforcement.</p> <p><strong>Status:</strong> <em>Contained, not solved. Cultural vitality remains a first-person responsibility of communities, not a deliverable of the framework.</em></p> <h4>2. Adversarial Adaptation</h4> <p><strong>The Tension:</strong> No matter how well-designed the immune system, actors will continuously attempt to game, mimic, or poison the system. Fake care contributions, coordinated LMCI manipulation, narrative hijacking (“surveillance socialism”), and AI-generated fraud will evolve faster than static defenses.</p> <p><strong>Containment Strategy:</strong> The GGF includes permanent adversarial functions: <strong>Office of the Adversary</strong> (independent red team), <strong>Planetary Immune System</strong> (systemic threat detection), <strong>AI Auditors</strong>, and <strong>Community Whistleblowing</strong> channels. The <strong>Legitimacy Drift Detector</strong> and <strong>Annual Accountable Coordination Audits</strong> provide ongoing sensing. However, adversarial adaptation is a <strong>co-evolutionary arms race</strong>, not a winnable war. The framework’s commitment is to <em>maintain the immune system as a permanent, funded function</em>, not to guarantee perfect security.</p> <p><strong>Status:</strong> <em>Permanently engaged. The absence of detected attacks is not evidence of security; it may be evidence of sophisticated concealment.</em></p> <h4>3. Optimization vs. Sacredness</h4> <p><strong>The Tension:</strong> LMCI-A measures impact. <em>Hearts</em> incentivize behavior. Systems adapt based on metrics. This creates a gravitational pull toward <strong>optimization of human behavior</strong>—a slow drift toward “economizing the soul.” At what point does “recognizing care” become “engineering people”?</p> <p><strong>Containment Strategy:</strong> The AUBI architecture contains this tension through <strong>constitutional firewalls</strong>:</p> <ul><li><strong>Layer 0</strong> is non-monetized by design. <em>Gratitude Tokens</em> in the Love Ledger are not convertible to <em>Hearts</em> or fiat.</li> <li>The <strong>Love Ledger</strong> and <strong>Hearts Treasury</strong> are architecturally separate systems with different validation chains.</li> <li><strong>Conscience, culture, and voluntary association</strong> (Genesis Protocol §1.5) explicitly limits GGF authority over personal conscience, cultural values, and spiritual sovereignty.</li></ul> <p>These boundaries do not “solve” the tension. They <strong>contain</strong> it, creating protected spaces where measurement and incentive do not reach. The framework acknowledges that some value must remain intentionally unoptimized, unrecognized, and sacred.</p> <p><strong>Status:</strong> <em>Structurally bounded. The tension will reassert itself continuously; the boundaries require ongoing vigilance.</em></p> <h4>4. Complexity Ceiling</h4> <p><strong>The Tension:</strong> The full GGF ecosystem is complex—necessarily so, because it anticipates capture, drift, and gaming across multiple scales. But complexity creates its own failure mode: partial implementation may distort the system, misunderstanding at lower governance levels may produce unintended consequences, and the framework may be perceived as inaccessible or technocratic.</p> <p><strong>Containment Strategy:</strong> Section 7.6 (“If You Only Implement 30%”) provides explicit minimum viable guidance. The <strong>BAZ Starter Pack</strong> bootstrapping pathway is designed to scale with demonstrated value, not to be deployed fully formed. The principle of <strong>Minimum Viable Governance</strong> (Treaty Core Principle 7) prioritizes the simplest mechanisms that achieve 80% of desired effects. Communities are explicitly instructed <em>not</em> to implement advanced layers until earlier stages are stable.</p> <p><strong>Status:</strong> <em>Managed through explicit sequencing and degradation pathways. The risk of fragile half-adoption remains, but the framework provides guardrails.</em></p> <h4>5. Emotional Legitimacy Under Crisis</h4> <p><strong>The Tension:</strong> Systems are judged not during stable operation, but during shocks, scandals, and failures. The real test is: <em>Does the public still trust the system when something goes wrong?</em> Rational accountability mechanisms (audits, tribunals) are necessary but insufficient; emotional legitimacy requires visible rituals of accountability and graceful failure.</p> <p><strong>Containment Strategy:</strong> The <strong>Deliberative Narrative Protocol</strong> <em>[Editorial note: no longer present in Genesis Protocol v0.9; upstream home undetermined]</em> ensures that after controversial decisions, a random sample of citizens produces a plain-language “Citizen’s Rationale” that appears <em>first</em> on public dashboards, before raw data. The <strong>Failure Library</strong> (Genesis Protocol §4.6) mandates public documentation of at least one failure before Phase 0 activation, normalizing learning from failure. <strong>Restorative justice circles</strong> and <strong>public testimony</strong> are encouraged as complements to formal audit processes. However, emotional legitimacy cannot be engineered; it must be <em>earned</em> through consistent, transparent, and humble practice.</p> <p><strong>Status:</strong> <em>Acknowledged as a first-person responsibility of governance actors. The framework provides scaffolding, not guarantees.</em></p> <h4>6. Observability vs. Surveillance</h4> <p><strong>The Tension:</strong> Adaptive systems need evidence about access, need, circulation, harm, and ecological change. The same infrastructures can become surveillance systems, especially when financial, relational, identity, migration, health, and community data are combined. More observation can reduce one form of blindness while producing chilling effects, strategic behaviour, cultural intrusion, or centralized power.</p> <p><strong>Containment Strategy:</strong> AUBI uses data minimization, purpose limitation, separate pipelines, individual and Indigenous data sovereignty, offline access, protected unobservability, the Love Ledger sacredness boundary, Observation Independence Maps, sensor-change governance, and the right to contest or withhold evidence where no overriding shared-commons harm is established. The system observes conditions necessary for support and coordination; it does not claim authority to render the whole person or community legible.</p> <p><strong>Status:</strong> <em>Structurally bounded but irreducible. The framework must repeatedly test whether its observation systems protect people from harm or primarily make them governable.</em></p> <hr> <p><strong>Why This Section Matters:</strong> Naming these irreducible tensions is not an admission of failure. It is a <strong>design specification for humility</strong>. The alternative—claiming to have “solved” these tensions—is the hallmark of brittle, capture-prone systems. The AUBI framework is offered as a <em>coordination technology</em>, not a utopian blueprint. Its resilience depends on users understanding its limits as clearly as its mechanisms.</p> <hr> <h2>Appendix A: Comprehensive Crisis & Failure Recovery</h2> <ul><li><strong>Economic Crises:</strong> <ul><li><strong>Inflation (>10%):</strong> Shift to baseline-only payouts, temporary wealth tax (1-2% on top 1%), P4 reserve deployment, Reform Accelerator Lab review within 60 days.</li> <li><strong>Recession:</strong> Increase AUBI payouts via counter-cyclical stabilizers, coordinate via P3.</li> <li><strong>Multi-Region Failure:</strong> Resource pooling, best practice sharing, coordinated recovery via P3.</li> <li><strong>International Contagion:</strong> P4 trade barriers, P3 mediation.</li></ul></li> <li><strong>Political Crises:</strong> <ul><li><strong>Authoritarian Capture:</strong> Governance distribution, Treaty oversight, community veto power.</li> <li><strong>Democratic Backsliding:</strong> Temporary Treaty governance, whistleblower visas.</li> <li><strong>Social Unrest:</strong> Community mediation, transparent communication.</li></ul></li> <li><strong>Technical Crises:</strong> <ul><li><strong>Security Breaches:</strong> Immediate isolation, backup activation, forensic investigation.</li> <li><strong>Blockchain Failures:</strong> Node redundancy (67% minimum), emergency consensus.</li> <li><strong>Hearts Instability:</strong> P4 reserve deployment, temporary fiat bridge.</li> <li><strong>Observation-System Compromise:</strong> Freeze state-specific interventions, preserve pre-incident evidence, activate independent verification, and publish the scope of affected decisions.</li> <li><strong>Mixed-State Failure:</strong> Disaggregate by subgroup, geography, provider, and wallet class; do not impose a uniform remedy until joint causes and sequencing are assessed.</li> <li><strong>Metric Self-Validation:</strong> Where an intervention directly moves its trigger indicator, require independent welfare and ecological review before continuation.</li></ul></li></ul> <hr> <h2>Appendix B: Political Economy & Stakeholder Strategy</h2> <ul><li><strong>Corporate Engagement:</strong> <ul><li><strong>Value Proposition:</strong> Access to care economy markets, anonymized Love Ledger insights, tax reductions (up to 5%) for LMCI-aligned initiatives.</li> <li><strong>Partnerships:</strong> Technology development, pilot sponsorship, employee volunteer programs.</li></ul></li> <li><strong>Labor Unions:</strong> <ul><li><strong>Integration:</strong> AUBI as supplement to union benefits, strike fund support, worker-owned cooperative development.</li> <li><strong>New Opportunities:</strong> Care work unionization, green job transitions.</li></ul></li> <li><strong>Political Parties:</strong> <ul><li><strong>Progressive:</strong> Social justice, environmental incentives.</li> <li><strong>Conservative:</strong> Economic freedom, local control.</li> <li><strong>Centrist:</strong> Pragmatic reform, economic stability.</li> <li><strong>Populist:</strong> Anti-elite measures, cultural protection.</li></ul></li></ul> <hr> <h2>Appendix C: Detailed Technical Architecture</h2> <ul><li><strong>Infrastructure:</strong> <ul><li><strong>Blockchain Platform:</strong> Hyperledger Fabric with Byzantine Fault Tolerance, 100+ nodes across 20 countries.</li> <li><strong>Transaction Capacity:</strong> 10,000+ transactions/second with sharding.</li> <li><strong>Data Storage:</strong> Hybrid on-chain/off-chain with IPFS.</li></ul></li> <li><strong>Scalability:</strong> <ul><li><strong>Sharding:</strong> Geographic sharding with cross-shard communication.</li> <li><strong>Layer 2 Solutions:</strong> State channels for microtransactions.</li> <li><strong>Load Balancing:</strong> Auto-scaling cloud infrastructure.</li></ul></li> <li><strong>Cybersecurity:</strong> <ul><li><strong>Threat Protection:</strong> Post-quantum cryptography, zero-trust architecture.</li> <li><strong>Fraud Prevention:</strong> AI-driven anomaly detection, multi-signature requirements.</li> <li><strong>Privacy:</strong> Data minimization, differential privacy, user-controlled settings.</li></ul></li> <li><strong>Disaster Recovery:</strong> <ul><li><strong>Redundancy:</strong> Data centers across continents, 99.99% uptime.</li> <li><strong>Backup:</strong> Real-time replication, 4-hour recovery time.</li></ul></li> <li><strong>Epistemic Infrastructure:</strong> <ul><li><strong>Provenance:</strong> Immutable framework, protocol, data, model, and correction versioning for consequential decisions.</li> <li><strong>Independence Mapping:</strong> Machine-readable declaration of shared observers, algorithms, platforms, funders, and incentives across channels.</li> <li><strong>Uncertainty Objects:</strong> Support for confidence ranges, missingness, disagreement, alternative hypotheses, and cause-unresolved status.</li> <li><strong>Rights Interface:</strong> Accessible evidence inspection, correction, contextual submission, human review, and appeal, with offline and assisted pathways.</li></ul></li></ul> <hr> <h2>Appendix D: Regional Implementation Case Studies</h2> <p><strong>Ficticious:</strong></p> <ul><li><strong>Indigenous Canada:</strong> <ul><li><strong>Base Amount:</strong> CAD 1,200, adjusted for remote areas.</li> <li><strong>Contributions:</strong> Traditional knowledge sharing, land stewardship (Leaves).</li> <li><strong>Verification:</strong> Indigenous councils, Decolonial Oversight, Love Ledger.</li> <li><strong>Cultural Adaptation:</strong> Nation-to-nation governance, ceremonial recognition.</li></ul></li> <li><strong>Sweden:</strong> <ul><li><strong>Base Amount:</strong> 15,000 SEK, taxable.</li> <li><strong>Contributions:</strong> Open-source IT, mindfulness teaching, youth internships.</li> <li><strong>Verification:</strong> BankID, offline options, Love Ledger.</li> <li><strong>Cultural Adaptation:</strong> Jantelagen respect, gender equality integration.</li></ul></li> <li><strong>Kenya:</strong> <ul><li><strong>Base Amount:</strong> 5,000 KES, rural/urban adjusted.</li> <li><strong>Contributions:</strong> Women’s savings groups, agroecology training.</li> <li><strong>Verification:</strong> Community elders, mobile platforms, Love Ledger.</li> <li><strong>Cultural Adaptation:</strong> Ubuntu philosophy, oral tradition preservation.</li></ul></li> <li><strong>Bangladesh:</strong> <ul><li><strong>Base Amount:</strong> 3,000 BDT, flood-prone adjustments.</li> <li><strong>Contributions:</strong> Women’s cooperatives, disaster preparedness.</li> <li><strong>Verification:</strong> NGO partnerships, community audits, Love Ledger.</li> <li><strong>Cultural Adaptation:</strong> Islamic finance compliance, women’s empowerment. <strong>Real</strong>:</li></ul></li> <li><strong>India (State-Level Care Transfers):</strong> <ul><li><strong>Context:</strong> The world’s largest care-recognition experiment, reaching 118 million women across 12 states (e.g., <em>Ladli Behna</em> in Madhya Pradesh, <em>Lakshmi Bhandar</em> in West Bengal).</li> <li><strong>Base Amount:</strong> 1,000–2,500 INR ($12–$30) monthly, unconditional.</li> <li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> Direct Benefit Transfer (DBT) utilizing India’s “Digital Public Infrastructure” (India Stack) for instant, low-cost delivery.</li> <li><strong>Validation:</strong> Proves the “Dignity Dividend.” Recipients report increased household decision-making power and reduced domestic conflict. Crucially, data refutes the “laziness” critique: receiving the transfer <em>did not</em> reduce participation in the paid labor force.</li> <li><strong>GGF Architectural Critique:</strong> <ul><li><strong>The Fiscal Trap:</strong> Current models pay for care using borrowed fiat currency, creating state-level deficits (e.g., half of participating states facing revenue shortfalls). <strong>Hearts</strong> integration would solve this by funding care with a closed-loop, demurrage-based currency that accelerates local velocity without spiking sovereign debt.</li> <li><strong>The Messaging Gap:</strong> Without a distinct mechanism like the <strong>Love Ledger</strong>, transfers are often perceived as “political vote-buying” rather than “earned compensation.” The GGF model explicitly links the currency to the labor of care, transforming the narrative from <em>Patronage</em> to <em>Right Relationship</em>.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul> <hr> <h2>Appendix E: Relocated Concepts</h2> <ul><li><strong>Pandemic Resilience Module:</strong> Moved to <strong>Global Health & Pandemic Security Framework</strong>.</li> <li><strong>Space Economy Bridge:</strong> Moved to <strong>Space Governance Framework</strong>.</li> <li><strong>Interfaith Governance Council:</strong> Moved to <strong>Religious & Spiritual Dialogue Framework</strong>.</li></ul> <h2>Appendix F: Protocol for Stateless, Nomadic, & Mobile Populations</h2> <h3>F.1. Recognized Categories</h3> <p>This protocol explicitly covers:</p> <ul><li><strong>Stateless Peoples:</strong> Including but not limited to the Romani, Rohingya, Nubians, some Kurds, and Palestinians.</li> <li><strong>Climate & Conflict Displaced:</strong> Refugees and internally displaced persons without formal status.</li> <li><strong>Nomadic & Pastoralist Communities:</strong> Such as the Mongols, Tuareg, and Bedouin.</li> <li><strong>The Unhoused:</strong> Individuals without a fixed address.</li></ul> <h3>F.2. Implementation Partnerships</h3> <ul><li><strong>UNHCR & IOM:</strong> For enrollment support and protection of refugees and stateless persons within their competent mandates; AUBI does not infer migration or legal status from partnership records.</li> <li><strong>Community-Based Organizations (CBOs):</strong> As possible trusted sources of purpose-limited community attestation and enrollment support (e.g., Romani organizations, homeless shelters), without acquiring general civil-identity authority.</li> <li><strong>Diaspora Networks:</strong> To facilitate enrollment support and continuity for dispersed communities where the claimant chooses and the relying AUBI body has valid authority.</li></ul> <h3>F.3. Cultural & Logistical Adaptation</h3> <ul><li><strong>Mobile Service Delivery:</strong> AUBI services must be deliverable to seasonal routes and temporary settlements without requiring a fixed address or universal identity credential.</li> <li><strong>Purpose-Limited Continuity:</strong> Movement across locations may require reconciliation of AUBI records, but such reconciliation follows §5.1.1, CIS v0.2.4, and Aurora v1.3.2 rather than creating a portable master identity.</li> <li><strong>Non-Monetized Contribution Recognition:</strong> For communities with strong gift-economy traditions, the <strong>Love Ledger</strong> recognizes non-monetized contributions (Layer 0) as equally valid as formal economic activity; those recognition records remain separate from AUBI identity and entitlement infrastructure.</li> <li><strong>Conflict Sensitivity:</strong> In regions with tense relationships between mobile and sedentary populations, AUBI implementation includes dialogue facilitation to prevent the program from becoming a source of new conflict.</li></ul> <hr> <h2>Appendix G: Observability and Epistemic Governance Protocols</h2> <h3>G.1. Observation Contract Template</h3> <p>Every consequential decision class records:</p> <ol><li>decision identifier, purpose, and authorized body;</li> <li>latent condition to be inferred;</li> <li>admissible raw observation channels;</li> <li>observer, data owner, and access authority;</li> <li>collection and sampling method;</li> <li>cadence, expected latency, and data age;</li> <li>known missingness and populations likely to remain unseen;</li> <li>shared-source and incentive dependencies;</li> <li>estimator or interpretive procedure;</li> <li>alternative states capable of generating the same observations;</li> <li>uncertainty and disagreement representation;</li> <li>threshold or decision rule;</li> <li>authorized action range and prohibited inferences;</li> <li>correction, challenge, review, and appeal rights;</li> <li>privacy, sacredness, and data-sovereignty restrictions;</li> <li>human, social, distributive, and ecological outcome measures;</li> <li>reassessment date, reversal condition, and sunset;</li> <li>framework and protocol versions.</li></ol> <h3>G.2. State Estimate Record Template</h3> <p><strong>Decision:</strong><br> <strong>As-of date:</strong><br> <strong>Framework/protocol versions:</strong><br> <strong>Estimated condition:</strong><br> <strong>Alternative hypotheses:</strong><br> <strong>Evidence supporting the estimate:</strong><br> <strong>Evidence contradicting the estimate:</strong><br> <strong>Data gaps and source dependencies:</strong><br> <strong>Confidence or partial-identification statement:</strong><br> <strong>Observer and community disagreement:</strong><br> <strong>Affected groups and their account:</strong><br> <strong>Selected action:</strong><br> <strong>Why the action remains acceptable under uncertainty:</strong><br> <strong>Required intervention order or preconditions:</strong><br> <strong>Independent expected outcomes:</strong><br> <strong>Reassessment date:</strong><br> <strong>Reversal or escalation condition:</strong></p> <h3>G.3. Individual Epistemic Rights Implementation</h3> <ul><li>Notice must arrive before adverse action except where immediate bounded action is necessary to prevent imminent harm; retrospective notice follows promptly.</li> <li>Evidence is presented in accessible, plain language and in the participant’s preferred available language and format.</li> <li>Participants may use an advocate, Community Weaver, trusted organization, or chosen representative without surrendering data ownership.</li> <li>Correction requests pause avoidable adverse action where the disputed fact is material.</li> <li>Appeals are heard by a body independent of the original decision chain.</li> <li>No appeal requires a Hearts bond for basic-income, AUBI access-attestation/person-continuity, needs, disability, fraud, or negative-adjustment cases.</li> <li>Reversal corrects downstream records and provides restoration where an erroneous decision caused harm.</li></ul> <h3>G.4. Missing-Population Sentinel</h3> <p>Administrative enrolment data observes people already coupled to AUBI. Each BAZ therefore uses privacy-preserving independent channels to estimate eligible but unenrolled or underserved populations:</p> <ul><li>trusted community and mutual-aid organizations;</li> <li>anonymous reasons-for-non-enrolment surveys;</li> <li>demographic discrepancy and service-access analysis;</li> <li>language, disability, digital, geographic, and documentation access audits;</li> <li>mobile, nomadic, displaced, unhoused, and institutionally confined population outreach;</li> <li>mystery-user and offline-access testing;</li> <li>reports of fear, surveillance concern, gatekeeping, or retaliation.</li></ul> <p>The sentinel reports ranges and uncertainty rather than creating a compulsory registry of the unseen.</p> <h3>G.5. Informal Care Burden Sentinel</h3> <p>This voluntary, claimant-controlled mechanism identifies when non-monetized care creates exhaustion, coercion, health effects, lost opportunity, material deprivation, or need for respite. It:</p> <ul><li>remains separate from LMCI-A and contribution scoring;</li> <li>does not convert Gratitude Tokens into economic entitlement or evidence of moral worth;</li> <li>may support a Layer 3 caregiving-burden assessment at the carer’s request;</li> <li>permits anonymous or trusted-intermediary access;</li> <li>records need and burden, not a comprehensive inventory of intimate relationships.</li></ul> <h3>G.6. Administrative Capacity Dashboard</h3> <p>Track at minimum:</p> <ul><li>Community Weaver caseload, supervision, turnover, and secondary-review access;</li> <li>enrolment, validation, Layer 3, correction, and appeal waiting times;</li> <li>unresolved disputes and tribunal delay;</li> <li>provider-chartering and audit backlogs;</li> <li>human-review percentage and false-positive anomaly investigations;</li> <li>language, disability, offline, and mobile-access performance;</li> <li>data-reconciliation failures and observation-channel conflicts;</li> <li>participant time and documentation burden;</li> <li>retaliation, complaint, and whistleblower-protection indicators.</li></ul> <p>Capacity failure is a system state requiring response. It must not be converted into presumed claimant non-compliance or fraud.</p> <h3>G.7. Observation Independence Map and Sensor-Change Record</h3> <p>For every major channel, publish whether it shares:</p> <ul><li>data source;</li> <li>observer or validator;</li> <li>algorithm or model;</li> <li>technical platform;</li> <li>funding body;</li> <li>governance authority;</li> <li>incentive or performance target;</li> <li>appeal or adjudication chain.</li></ul> <p>A sensor-change record states the previous and new method, expected information gain, information lost, affected historical comparability, groups likely to be advantaged or obscured, public objections, and rollback condition.</p> <h3>G.8. Low Hearts Velocity Minimum Diagnostic Set</h3> <p>A Stage 3 BAZ maintains the smallest practical portfolio capable of separating major intervention classes:</p> <ul><li>unmet demand and failed requests;</li> <li>provider capacity, waiting time, redundancy, and coverage;</li> <li>essential Hearts acceptance and fiat-essential burden;</li> <li>wallet and dormancy concentration;</li> <li>independent reasons-for-non-use sampling;</li> <li>technical and offline reconciliation;</li> <li>seasonal baseline;</li> <li>material sufficiency, informal substitution, and informal-carer burden.</li></ul> <p>Additional sensing must demonstrate decision value greater than its financial, administrative, privacy, cultural, and latency costs. Where a retuned decision rule using existing information performs as well, the new sensor should not be added.</p>",1);function c(e){var t=a();n(850),r(e,t)}export{c as default};
